---
/**
 * The arena — build steps 0 to 5 of `docs/cat-boss-gdd.md` §12.
 *
 * **Step 0**: wire the toggle in the cat's HUD (rendered by SiteCat.astro) to an
 * instant swap. No transition, no fade — the §14 ink curtain is deliberately the
 * *last* thing built, and this instant path stays as its permanent fallback.
 *
 * **Step 1**: Claim + Scrub. Hold the cursor still on a block the cat has taken and
 * it comes back to you.
 *
 * **Step 2**: Pounce. The cat leaves the footer, stalks the cursor, and interrupts a
 * scrub it judges worth interrupting — telegraph, leap, recover.
 *
 * **Step 3**: Treats. Click to spend one of the treats you found while browsing; the
 * cat abandons everything to go and eat it, and cannot pounce while it does.
 *
 * **Step 4**: The loop closes — a 3px territory bar on the top edge, two endings that
 * mean different things, and a cat that talks. Winning marks its ear for the session.
 *
 * **Step 5**: Replayability. The cat picks a **stance** per fight (§9.3) — same verbs,
 * different opponent — and treats stop being interchangeable: which pages you read decides
 * what your five shapes actually *do* (§9.5).
 *
 * What all of it does to the page, and nothing more: adds a class and one custom
 * property to some existing elements. Claims never affect layout and never hide
 * anything (§11), so text stays selectable and every link stays clickable while the
 * cat "holds" the block around it.
 *
 * The shape of the loop, now that all three exist: **a pounce always beats a scrub it
 * commits to** (it lands at ~84% of the hold), so ground is taken where the cat isn't
 * — and a treat is how you *make* somewhere the cat isn't. Ammo comes only from having
 * browsed the site (pillar 5), so a visitor who opens a fight cold has none, and the
 * tab bar is the tutorial.
 *
 * Off is the only state a page can load in. Nothing is written to storage.
 */
---

{/* The curtain (§14). Full-screen, on top of everything, and inert: a click during the
    transition is swallowed rather than landing on a half-claimed page. Hidden except
    during the ~1.7s it is running, so it costs a page that never opens a fight nothing
    but an empty element. */}
<canvas id="cat-curtain" transition:persist="cat-curtain" aria-hidden="true" hidden></canvas>

{/* Territory (§6): 3px on the top edge, no numbers.
    The cat pushes up from the bottom of the page, so the top edge is *your* edge, and
    the filled part is your share. A percentage would invite optimising a thing that is
    supposed to be felt — and there is nowhere on a portfolio for a scoreboard. */}
<div id="cat-territory" transition:persist="cat-territory" aria-hidden="true" hidden>
  <span class="territory-mine"></span>
</div>

{/* What the cat says (§8). Anchored above the cat and follows it — never centre-screen,
    which would cover the portfolio, which is the actual product. `aria-hidden` like the
    rest of the cat: nothing here carries information the player needs. */}
<div id="cat-ribbon" transition:persist="cat-ribbon" aria-hidden="true" hidden></div>

{/* A thrown treat. Its own element rather than SiteCat's `#cat-treat`: the shapes
    live in a shared <defs> block, so this reuses the art by reference without
    borrowing any of that element's state — no clobbering the treat a page is
    currently hiding, and no interference with the credit SiteCat owes it.

    `pointer-events: none` for the same reason the other one has it: a decorative
    treat has no business eating a click meant for a link. */}
<div id="cat-throw" transition:persist="cat-throw" aria-hidden="true" hidden>
  <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="20" height="20" aria-hidden="true">
    <use href="#treat-fish"></use>
  </svg>
</div>

{/* The squad's home (§15, 2.0).

    Empty in the markup and filled at runtime with clones of the cat's own drawing —
    §0 forbids new art, and `SiteCat.astro` records why a hand-built element is the
    wrong tool here: Astro scopes styles to what it renders, so a `createElement`'d
    paw "never matched `.cat-paw` and came out 0x0 and invisible". A clone carries the
    scope class with it, so a kitten is literally the same cat, smaller.

    It exists as a rendered container for one reason: `transition:persist`. The
    kittens are created by a script whose module scope survives a client-side
    navigation, so their DOM has to survive it too, or the squad would evaporate on
    a link click while the fight it belongs to carried on.

    `aria-hidden` and `pointer-events: none` like everything else the fight draws:
    the game carries no information (§11) and must never eat a click meant for the
    page under it. */}
<div id="cat-squad" transition:persist="cat-squad" aria-hidden="true"></div>

{/* The scrub ring: a cursor-sized dial that fills while you hold still. Fixed,
    aria-hidden and pointer-events: none — it must never be the thing under the
    cursor, or it would eat the hover it is reporting on. */}
<div id="cat-scrub" transition:persist="cat-scrub" aria-hidden="true" hidden>
  <svg viewBox="0 0 40 40" width="40" height="40">
    <circle class="scrub-track" cx="20" cy="20" r="17" fill="none" stroke-width="2.5"></circle>
    <circle
      class="scrub-fill"
      cx="20"
      cy="20"
      r="17"
      fill="none"
      stroke-width="2.5"
      stroke-linecap="round"
      transform="rotate(-90 20 20)"></circle>
  </svg>
</div>

<style>
  /* ---- a claimed element ----
     §5.1 asked for "desaturated, tilted, paw watermark". Built and looked at, and
     the first two do nothing here: this page is cream and near-black, so
     `saturate(0.35)` is invisible on it, and 0.5° of tilt is a rounding error. A
     claim you cannot see is not a claim — there is nothing to scrub *toward*, which
     is the fun hypothesis failing for a reason that has nothing to do with the
     mechanic. What reads on a monochrome page is a wash and an edge:

     - `box-shadow: inset` paints a flat tint over the element's whole box, above
       its background and **below its text**, so the block is visibly taken without
       the words getting harder to read.
     - `outline` draws the boundary, and — the reason it is allowed here at all —
       outlines are painted, never laid out. Same guarantee §11 wanted from
       "transform and filter only": nothing moves, nothing is hidden.

     The wash is 7%, not the 12% it read best at: 12% put `.rail` — small, mono,
     `--color-muted` — at 4.15:1 against its own tinted background, under AA's 4.5,
     and §11 makes that a gate rather than a trade. So the edge does the shouting
     and the wash only murmurs. The tilt stays as a garnish, capped in `arena.ts`. */
  :global(.cat-claimed) {
    transform: rotate(var(--claim-tilt, 0deg)) scale(0.994);
    box-shadow: inset 0 0 0 100vmax color-mix(in srgb, var(--color-accent) 7%, transparent);
    outline: 2px dashed color-mix(in srgb, var(--color-accent) 62%, transparent);
    outline-offset: 3px;
    /* the pointer is the tool, so a claim says it is a target */
    cursor: crosshair;
  }

  /* ---- touch (§5.2, answered in 1.2) ----
     Three properties, only on claimed elements, only while claimed, and removed with the class.

     A finger held on a claimed *link* for 1400ms is a long-press: iOS raises its link preview,
     Android its context menu, and either one eats the core verb. And any held finger is a
     candidate scroll, so the page slides out from under the hold.

     `touch-action: pinch-zoom` and **not** `none`: `none` would refuse a pinch that begins on a
     claim, and §11 makes zoom a no-exceptions row. This refuses panning only — which is the
     thing that was stealing the hold — and leaves the gesture an accessibility requirement
     depends on.

     None of the three affects layout, which is what §11's rule was restated in 0.3 to admit. */
  @media (hover: none), (pointer: coarse) {
    :global(.cat-claimed) {
      -webkit-touch-callout: none;
      user-select: none;
      -webkit-user-select: none;
      touch-action: pinch-zoom;
      /* no cursor to be a crosshair; the claim itself is the affordance */
      cursor: auto;
    }
    /* The hold's progress, shown *in the claim* rather than at the finger that is covering it.
       Reuses §5.1's own wash — the same `--color-accent` inset, scaled from the 7% of a claim up
       toward the 26% the `cat-freed` drain starts at, so a filling claim is visibly on its way to
       being released and arrives at exactly the tint the release animation begins from. `--scrub`
       is set per-frame by `showScrub` and cleared by `hideRing`. */
    :global(.cat-claimed) {
      box-shadow: inset 0 0 0 100vmax
        color-mix(
          in srgb,
          var(--color-accent) calc(7% + 19% * var(--scrub, 0)),
          transparent
        );
    }
  }
  /* The moment it comes back to you: the wash drains out rather than blinking off.
     Kept off under reduced motion like everything else, though the arena itself
     does not open there yet. */
  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: no-preference) {
    :global(.cat-freed) {
      animation: cat-freed 0.42s ease-out;
    }
    @keyframes cat-freed {
      from {
        box-shadow: inset 0 0 0 100vmax color-mix(in srgb, var(--color-accent) 26%, transparent);
      }
      to {
        box-shadow: inset 0 0 0 100vmax transparent;
      }
    }
  }

  /* While a fight is on, the root clips horizontally. Belt and braces beside
     MAX_TILT: `clip` (not `hidden`) creates no scroll container, so the sticky
     header and every anchor jump still work. An easter egg does not get to give
     the page a horizontal scrollbar. */
  :global(html.cat-arena-on) {
    overflow-x: clip;
    /*
     * Crosshair = you can put a treat here. It is the whole affordance for §5.4, and
     * it costs nothing: links and buttons keep `cursor: pointer` from the UA
     * stylesheet, because a declaration on the element beats an inherited one. So the
     * cursor draws the one boundary the player needs — **crosshair throws, pointer
     * navigates** — with no new UI and nothing added to the page.
     *
     * It matters more than it looks. A portfolio is mostly links, and a click on one
     * still navigates (§11 promises the page keeps working), which ends the fight. The
     * cursor is what turns that from a mystery into a rule.
     */
    cursor: crosshair;
  }

  /* ---- a treat the ladder is holding back (§9.4) ----
     A third state for a 9px dot, and it needs to be a third one rather than a reuse. Unfilled
     already means "not found" outside a fight and "spent" inside one; a withheld treat is
     neither, and the visitor is being asked to plan around exactly this number.

     Hollow rather than dimmed: the accent says *yours*, and the missing fill says *not in
     hand*. Dimming would read as a rendering artefact at this size, and a different hue would
     be a new colour for one dot. Same shape, no new art (§0). */
  :global(#cat-score .cat-paw.withheld) {
    background: transparent;
    box-shadow: inset 0 0 0 1.5px color-mix(in srgb, var(--color-accent) 70%, transparent);
  }

  /* ---- the cat, while the arena has it (§5.3) ----
     `SiteCat.astro` owns this element; the arena borrows it via `cat:standdown` and
     writes only its transform. These rules are the phases, and they are all on the
     inner `.cat-svg` so they compose with the positioning transform rather than
     fighting it.

     `pointer-events: none` on the body is load-bearing twice over: a boss is not a
     pet, so it must not answer a boop mid-fight — and a cat parked on the cursor
     would otherwise be what `elementFromPoint` returns, which would silently make
     scrubbing impossible at exactly the moment the game gets interesting. */
  :global(#site-cat.boss) {
    z-index: 42;
  }
  :global(#site-cat.boss .cat-svg) {
    pointer-events: none;
    transform-origin: center bottom;
    transition: transform 0.12s ease-out;
  }
  /* wind-up: gather, ears back, hold. This is the 420ms you get to read. */
  :global(#site-cat.boss-telegraph .cat-svg) {
    transform: scale(1.06, 0.82);
  }
  :global(#site-cat.boss-telegraph .cat-ear) {
    transform: rotate(-24deg);
  }
  :global(#site-cat.boss-telegraph .cat-tail) {
    transform: rotate(-18deg);
  }
  /* The tell (§9.3). A real wind-up drops low and flattens its ears; a bluff gathers
     upright and keeps them half up. It is a small difference on purpose — §9.3 says the
     tell can be *learned*, which means it has to be missable the first few times. */
  :global(#site-cat.boss-feint .cat-svg) {
    transform: scale(1.02, 0.94);
  }
  :global(#site-cat.boss-feint .cat-ear) {
    transform: rotate(-9deg);
  }
  :global(#site-cat.boss-feint .cat-tail) {
    transform: rotate(-4deg);
  }

  /* in the air: stretched along the direction of travel */
  :global(#site-cat.boss-leap .cat-svg) {
    transform: scale(1.14, 1.06);
    transition: none;
  }
  /* Swatted (§5.4's counter, 1.4) — squashed flatter than a plain recovery and tipped the other
     way, so the player can see that the thing they did landed.

     It has to out-read `boss-recover`, which is the state it extends: a recovering cat is already
     slumped, so "recovering for longer" is invisible without a distinct pose. Flatter and rotated
     the opposite direction reads as *knocked* rather than as *landed*, which is the difference
     between the cat's mistake and the player's hit. Scale and rotation only, like every other
     boss pose (§0's art constraint, as revised in 1.3). */
  :global(#site-cat.boss-swatted .cat-svg) {
    transform: scale(1.1, 0.78) rotate(5deg);
  }
  :global(#site-cat.boss-swatted .cat-ear) {
    transform: rotate(-30deg);
  }
  /* landed on a paragraph, briefly regretting it */
  :global(#site-cat.boss-recover .cat-svg) {
    transform: scale(1.04, 0.9) rotate(-4deg);
  }
  :global(#site-cat.boss-recover .cat-tail) {
    transform: rotate(12deg);
  }
  /* head down in something better than you */
  :global(#site-cat.boss-eat .cat-svg) {
    transform: scale(1.02, 0.95);
  }
  :global(#site-cat.boss-eat .cat-head) {
    transform: translateY(3.5px);
  }
  :global(#site-cat.boss-eat .cat-tail) {
    transform: rotate(-6deg);
  }

  /* ---- 1.3: the boss looks like a boss ----
     `data-mood` (3 tiers) and `data-stance` (4 stances) have been published on the cat
     since step 5, and until now no CSS read them — eight opponent states only a harness
     could see, and a boss that was the same 48×30 silhouette as the ambient cat. The
     levers, per §0's constraint: scale (the whole animal, via `.cat-flip` so the phase
     transforms on `.cat-svg` stay theirs), colour (the SVG is `fill="currentColor"`, so
     one property recolours the whole animal), glow (`drop-shadow`), and posture
     (`transform` on `.cat-tail` / `.cat-ear` / `.cat-eye`) — never internal detail.

     Posture is gated on **not** being in a phase: the telegraph's ears-back tell is a
     phase rule below, and a mood that flattened the ears all the time would make the
     feint unreadable. Mood and stance pose the *resting* cat; phases override while they
     run. */

  /* bigger than the cat, and growing with territory so the escalation is readable.
     Scaled on `.cat-flip` (SiteCat's face-flip wrapper) so `placeCat`'s positioning math
     — which reads the element box — never moves. */
  :global(#site-cat.boss .cat-flip) {
    transform: scale(var(--boss-scale, 1.5));
    transform-origin: center bottom;
  }
  :global(#site-cat.boss.face-left .cat-flip) {
    transform: scaleX(-1) scale(var(--boss-scale, 1.5));
  }
  /* the eye needs a transform-box before any posture can touch it */
  :global(#site-cat.boss .cat-eye) {
    transform-box: fill-box;
    transform-origin: center;
  }

  /* mood — the tiers are behaviour, and now they have a face too */
  :global(#site-cat.boss[data-mood='bored']) {
    /* losing interest: dimmer ink, no glow */
    color: color-mix(in srgb, var(--color-ink) 60%, var(--color-muted));
  }
  :global(#site-cat.boss[data-mood='desperate']) {
    /* wound up: ledger-wine silhouette with a hot glow */
    color: var(--color-accent);
    filter: drop-shadow(0 0 6px color-mix(in srgb, var(--color-accent) 55%, transparent));
  }
  /* stance — the cat's posture at rest, per opponent */
  :global(#site-cat.boss:not(.boss-telegraph):not(.boss-feint):not(.boss-leap):not(.boss-recover):not(.boss-eat):not(.boss-fetch)[data-stance='ambush']) .cat-tail {
    transform: rotate(-22deg) translateY(2px); /* tucked, lying low */
  }
  :global(#site-cat.boss:not(.boss-telegraph):not(.boss-feint):not(.boss-leap):not(.boss-recover):not(.boss-eat):not(.boss-fetch)[data-stance='ambush']) .cat-ear {
    transform: rotate(-12deg); /* half-back, watching */
  }
  :global(#site-cat.boss:not(.boss-telegraph):not(.boss-feint):not(.boss-leap):not(.boss-recover):not(.boss-eat):not(.boss-fetch)[data-stance='siege']) .cat-tail {
    transform: rotate(6deg); /* planted, braced */
  }
  :global(#site-cat.boss:not(.boss-telegraph):not(.boss-feint):not(.boss-leap):not(.boss-recover):not(.boss-eat):not(.boss-fetch)[data-stance='trickster']) .cat-tail {
    transform: rotate(-8deg); /* restless, mid-fidget */
  }
  :global(#site-cat.boss:not(.boss-telegraph):not(.boss-feint):not(.boss-leap):not(.boss-recover):not(.boss-eat):not(.boss-fetch)[data-stance='sleepy']) .cat-ear {
    transform: rotate(28deg); /* drooped */
  }
  :global(#site-cat.boss:not(.boss-telegraph):not(.boss-feint):not(.boss-leap):not(.boss-recover):not(.boss-eat):not(.boss-fetch)[data-stance='sleepy']) .cat-eye {
    transform: scaleY(0.25); /* half-lidded — barely interested */
  }
  /* mood posture, same phase gate — the tier is a stance on top of the stance */
  :global(#site-cat.boss:not(.boss-telegraph):not(.boss-feint):not(.boss-leap):not(.boss-recover):not(.boss-eat):not(.boss-fetch)[data-mood='bored']) .cat-ear {
    transform: rotate(14deg);
  }
  :global(#site-cat.boss:not(.boss-telegraph):not(.boss-feint):not(.boss-leap):not(.boss-recover):not(.boss-eat):not(.boss-fetch)[data-mood='bored']) .cat-tail {
    transform: rotate(-16deg) translateY(1px);
  }
  :global(#site-cat.boss:not(.boss-telegraph):not(.boss-feint):not(.boss-leap):not(.boss-recover):not(.boss-eat):not(.boss-fetch)[data-mood='desperate']) .cat-ear {
    transform: rotate(-30deg); /* pinned flat back */
  }
  :global(#site-cat.boss:not(.boss-telegraph):not(.boss-feint):not(.boss-leap):not(.boss-recover):not(.boss-eat):not(.boss-fetch)[data-mood='desperate']) .cat-tail {
    transform: rotate(26deg); /* hackles up */
  }
  :global(#site-cat.boss:not(.boss-telegraph):not(.boss-feint):not(.boss-leap):not(.boss-recover):not(.boss-eat):not(.boss-fetch)[data-mood='desperate']) .cat-eye {
    transform: scale(1.45); /* wide — the tell §7.3 promised and never had */
  }

  /* the trickster's tail twitch — the only piece that moves, so it is motion-gated */
  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: no-preference) {
    :global(#site-cat.boss:not(.boss-telegraph):not(.boss-feint):not(.boss-leap):not(.boss-recover):not(.boss-eat):not(.boss-fetch)[data-stance='trickster'] .cat-tail) {
      animation: cat-twitch 0.9s ease-in-out infinite;
    }
    :global(#site-cat.boss .cat-flip) {
      transition: transform 0.45s ease-out;
    }
    @keyframes cat-twitch {
      0%,
      100% {
        transform: rotate(-8deg);
      }
      50% {
        transform: rotate(2deg);
      }
    }
  }

  /* ---- the curtain (§14) ---- */
  #cat-curtain {
    position: fixed;
    inset: 0;
    /*
     * Explicit, and load-bearing. The backing store is sized in *grid* cells rather than
     * device pixels (see `runCurtain`), so the element has an intrinsic size of a few hundred
     * pixels — and an absolutely positioned replaced element with `width: auto` takes its
     * intrinsic width and drops the `right` offset on the floor. `inset: 0` alone would leave
     * a 256×180 curtain in the corner of the screen.
     */
    width: 100%;
    height: 100%;
    z-index: 60;
    /* Never the thing under the cursor: a click aimed at the page mid-transition should
       be swallowed, not delivered to a page that is halfway through being claimed. */
    pointer-events: none;
    /*
     * The colour the screen floods toward, and it **darkens in both themes** (§14.4). In
     * light mode that is the ink. In dark mode the ink is a pale pink, and a full-screen
     * pale sheet over a dark page is a flashbang — so dark mode floods toward a ground
     * deepened almost to black instead. One luminance reversal, downward, either way.
     */
    --curtain-ink: #2a241e;
  }
  :global(html.dark) #cat-curtain {
    --curtain-ink: #0b0908;
  }
  #cat-curtain[hidden] {
    display: none;
  }

  /* ---- territory, on the top edge ---- */
  #cat-territory {
    position: fixed;
    inset: 0 0 auto 0;
    height: 3px;
    z-index: 46;
    pointer-events: none;
    background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--color-ink) 14%, transparent);
  }
  #cat-territory[hidden] {
    display: none;
  }
  .territory-mine {
    display: block;
    height: 100%;
    width: 0;
    background: var(--color-accent);
    /* Wide enough to read as movement, short enough that a reclaim feels immediate. The
       transition is the only thing that makes a 3px bar noticeable at all. */
    transition: width 0.45s ease-out;
  }
  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
    .territory-mine {
      transition: none;
    }
  }

  /* ---- the ribbon ---- */
  #cat-ribbon {
    position: fixed;
    left: 0;
    top: 0;
    z-index: 43;
    max-width: 15rem;
    padding: 0.28rem 0.5rem;
    border: 1px solid var(--color-line);
    border-radius: var(--radius-chip);
    background: var(--color-ground);
    color: var(--color-ink);
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 0.72rem;
    line-height: 1.35;
    text-wrap: pretty;
    pointer-events: none;
    opacity: 0;
    transition: opacity 0.2s ease-out;
  }
  #cat-ribbon[hidden] {
    display: none;
  }
  #cat-ribbon.show {
    opacity: 0.96;
  }

  /* ---- a thrown treat ----
     Positioned exactly like the cat is: pinned to the bottom-left corner, moved by
     transform only, so one helper places both. */
  #cat-throw {
    position: fixed;
    left: 0;
    bottom: 0;
    z-index: 41;
    width: 20px;
    height: 20px;
    margin: -10px 0 0 -10px;
    pointer-events: none;
    color: var(--color-accent);
    opacity: 0.9;
  }
  #cat-throw[hidden] {
    display: none;
  }
  #cat-throw svg {
    display: block;
  }
  /* The cat has its head down on it — a small settle so the treat reads as being
     eaten rather than as having vanished. */
  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: no-preference) {
    #cat-throw.eaten {
      animation: cat-treat-gone 0.4s ease-in forwards;
    }
    @keyframes cat-treat-gone {
      to {
        opacity: 0;
        transform: var(--throw-at) scale(0.4);
      }
    }
  }

  /* ---- The squad (§15, 2.0) ------------------------------------------------
     A kitten is the cat's own drawing at 62%, so nothing new is drawn (§0). These
     rules are `:global` because the elements are cloned and appended at runtime and
     therefore carry no scope class of this component's — the same reason every rule
     this file writes about `#site-cat` is global. */
  #cat-squad {
    position: fixed;
    left: 0;
    top: 0;
    width: 0;
    height: 0;
    /* Under the ring and the ribbon, over the page: a kitten is a character in the
       fight, not chrome, and it must never be the thing a click lands on. */
    z-index: 39;
    pointer-events: none;
  }
  :global(.cat-kit) {
    position: fixed;
    left: 0;
    top: 0;
    /* The drawing is 48×30 and the transform scales about its own top-left, so this
       centres the animal on the coordinate the script thinks it is standing on. */
    margin: -9px 0 0 -15px;
    color: var(--color-ink);
    opacity: 0.82;
    pointer-events: none;
    will-change: transform;
    /* The same paper halo the cat wears, so a kitten stays readable over a dark cover. */
    filter: drop-shadow(0 0 2px var(--color-ground));
  }
  /* Caught by a pounce (§15) — a flinch, not a stun. Squashed, borrowing the boss's own
     `boss-swatted` vocabulary on purpose: both animals show being caught out the same
     way, so a visitor learns one shape and reads both. The kitten keeps working
     through it, because the arithmetic in `KITTEN_FLINCH_MS` says it must. */
  :global(.cat-kit.kit-hit) {
    transform-origin: 50% 100%;
    filter: drop-shadow(0 0 2px var(--color-ground)) saturate(0.6);
    opacity: 0.6;
  }
  /* Just been given an order — a single pulse of the accent, so the visitor can see
     *which* kitten heard them. Colour only: nothing here may affect layout (§11). */
  :global(.cat-kit.kit-called) {
    color: var(--color-accent);
  }
  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: no-preference) {
    :global(.cat-kit) {
      transition: color 220ms ease-out, opacity 220ms ease-out;
    }
  }

  #cat-scrub {
    position: fixed;
    left: 0;
    top: 0;
    z-index: 41;
    width: 40px;
    height: 40px;
    margin: -20px 0 0 -20px;
    pointer-events: none;
    color: var(--color-accent);
    will-change: transform;
  }
  #cat-scrub svg {
    display: block;
    overflow: visible;
  }
  .scrub-track {
    stroke: color-mix(in srgb, var(--color-ink) 22%, transparent);
  }
  .scrub-fill {
    stroke: currentColor;
    /* 2πr for r=17, set here so the script only ever writes the offset */
    stroke-dasharray: 106.81;
    stroke-dashoffset: 106.81;
  }
</style>

<script>
  import { motionReduced } from '@/lib/a11y-prefs';
  import { runCurtain, stop as stopCurtain, HOLD_FLOOR_MS, REVEAL_MS } from '@/lib/curtain';
  import { tallyFor, resolveTreat } from '@/lib/cat-game';
  import { playCue, setSfxEnabled, sfxEnabled } from '@/lib/cat-sfx';
  import {
    CLAIMABLE,
    PROTECTED,
    PROTECTED_TREE,
    INTERACTIVE,
    INITIAL_CLAIM_FRACTION,
    HIDDEN_TRUCE_MS,
    IDLE_TRUCE_MS,
    AIM_LEAD_MS,
    LEAP_MS,
    OPENING_GRACE_MS,
    OPENING_LINE_MS,
    RECLAIM_ON_HIT,
    STALK_SPEED,
    THROW_ARC_MS,
    LURE_MS,
    FETCH_SPEED,
    FETCH_REACH,
    bigEnough,
    dropNested,
    boardSlice,
    pickClaims,
    STANCES,
    pickStance,
    treatSpec,
    type Stance,
    nextRung,
    ammoCap,
    mood as moodFor,
    aggression,
    telegraphScale,
    swatLands,
    SWAT_STUN_MS,
    sweepPartner,
    SIEGE_SWEEP_EVERY,
    AMBUSH_PIN_MS,
    AMBUSH_PIN_PX,
    TRICKSTER_DOUBLE,
    patienceFor,
    talkGap,
    GROOM_EVERY_MS,
    GROOM_MS,
    type Mood,
    claimTilt,
    scrubProgress,
    stillEnough,
    isTap,
    isWorking,
    arenaCaption,
    territory,
    isWon,
    isLost,
    LINE_MS,
    WIN_BEAT_MS,
    ROUND_BEAT_MS,
    LOSE_BEAT_MS,
    pickLine,
    lineText,
    type FightState,
    nextPhase,
    canPounce,
    provoked,
    predict,
    leapPos,
    pounceHit,
    type Phase,
  } from '@/lib/arena';
  import {
    KITTEN_SPEED,
    KITTEN_FLINCH_MS,
    KITTEN_WORK_MS,
    ARRIVE_PX,
    WATCH_TRUCE_MS,
    canFinish,
    kittensFor,
    pickWork,
    assignOrder,
    roundOutcome,
    roundRegrowMs,
    roundAggro,
    readBestRound,
    writeBestRound,
    type Candidate,
  } from '@/lib/squad';

  /**
   * Which game this is (§15, 2.0).
   *
   * **Commander** is the default and the reason 2.0 exists: kittens perform §5.2's hold and the
   * visitor gives orders, or gives nothing at all and watches. **Manual** is 1.4's fight, kept
   * whole — the three verbs, the endings, §9.4's ladder — for anyone who wants to play it, and
   * kept honest by the fourteen harnesses that measure it.
   *
   * Session-only, like everything else about a fight except the one stored round (§13.4). A
   * refresh comes back a commander, because that is the mode a visitor who has never chosen
   * should meet.
   */
  type Mode = 'commander' | 'manual';
  let mode: Mode = 'commander';
  const commanding = () => mode === 'commander';

  /**
   * Live state, module scope, session only.
   *
   * `on` is never read from anywhere but here, which is the asymmetry §13.4 was
   * reaching for: a page can only ever *load* with the arena off, so a visitor
   * can never arrive mid-invasion — not after a refresh, not on a return visit,
   * not from a shared link.
   */
  const arena = {
    on: false,
    /** Elements the cat currently holds, in DOM order. */
    claimed: [] as HTMLElement[],
    /** Every element it ever held, so a landed pounce has something to take back. */
    freed: [] as HTMLElement[],
    /** The board as queried, so a re-claim can find an element's original index. */
    order: [] as HTMLElement[],
    /** How many it started with, so the HUD line has a denominator. */
    total: 0,
    /** This fight's composition seed — re-claims must not re-roll the tilt. */
    seed: 0,
    /** When the fight began, for the opening grace (§7.4). */
    openedAt: 0,
    /**
     * The one treat allowed on the board, in flight or waiting to be eaten.
     *
     * One at a time is what keeps lures from being banked, now that a badly timed
     * throw waits to be useful instead of evaporating.
     */
    treat: null as null | {
      x: number;
      y: number;
      fx: number;
      fy: number;
      at: number;
      landed: boolean;
      /** Which shape, and so which effects — §9.5's loadout. */
      kind: string;
    },
    /** Which paws were filled when the fight opened, so spending is fight-local. */
    ammoWas: [] as boolean[],
    /**
     * Which rung of §9.4's ladder the next fight will be fought on.
     *
     * Module scope, like the rest of `arena` — Astro runs this script once per document, so
     * the rung survives moving between pages and dies on a refresh. That is the same lifetime
     * as SiteCat's found-set and exactly the one §7.2 asks for, and it costs nothing to get:
     * there is no storage to write and nothing a returning visitor inherits.
     */
    rung: 0,
    /** What the borrowed HUD line should say — see `holdCaption`. */
    caption: '',
    /** Treats thrown this fight, which the cat has opinions about (§8.4). */
    spent: 0,
    /** Consecutive interrupted scrubs, reset by a completed one. */
    interrupts: 0,
    /** When territory last changed, for the "nothing is happening" lines. */
    movedAt: 0,
    /** The line currently up, and when it went up. */
    lastLine: '' as string,
    spokeAt: 0,
    lineTimer: 0,
    /** The ending beat's own timer, so a stale one cannot close the *next* fight. */
    beatTimer: 0,
    /** Set once an ending has started, so nothing can end twice. */
    ending: null as null | 'win' | 'lose',
    /**
     * What the visitor has *asked* for, which is not the same as what is running.
     *
     * Step 6 opened a window — the length of the flood — in which the fight is committed to
     * but not yet on. Everything used to branch on `on`, so a second press inside that window
     * read as "not in a fight, start one": it aborted the curtain, opened the arena through
     * the abort path, and then opened it *again* when the second curtain reached its hold.
     * Two `open()`s with no `close()` between them replace `arena.claimed` wholesale and the
     * first board's claims are stranded on the page. Six presses at six beats left 12.
     *
     * So intent is what the toggle and Esc branch on, and it is set the instant the press
     * lands. This is also the better answer for §13.5: while a curtain is up, what the button
     * should say is what you asked it for.
     */
    want: false,
    /** Reverses the running curtain from wherever it has got to (§14.6). */
    curtain: null as null | (() => void),
    /** rAF handle for the loop. */
    raf: 0,
    /**
     * Which round of commander mode this is, counting from 1 (§15).
     *
     * Rounds are the endless mode's shape: clearing one deals a fresh board on the same page,
     * with a shorter regrow clock, a meaner cat and one more kitten. Losing the board does not
     * lower it, because there is nothing to lose — the round simply starts again.
     */
    round: 1,
    /** When this round's board was dealt, so a clear has a time to report. */
    roundAt: 0,
    /** Deepest round ever reached, read once from storage — the only thing that persists. */
    best: 0,
  };

  /**
   * Whatever the boss is hunting this frame (§15).
   *
   * The boss does not know or care what it is stalking: it wants a position, a velocity to lead,
   * and something whose hold it can break when it lands. In **manual** mode that is the pointer;
   * in **commander** mode it is the nearest kitten, and the cursor is never a target — which is
   * what lets a visitor read the page while a fight happens on it.
   *
   * A reference rather than a copy, so `land()` clearing `quarry.target` clears the *right*
   * hold. Re-pointed once a frame in `tick`, before anything reads it.
   */
  interface Prey {
    x: number;
    y: number;
    ax: number;
    ay: number;
    vx: number;
    vy: number;
    at: number;
    since: number;
    inside: boolean;
    target: HTMLElement | null;
  }

  /** Scrub tracking: where the pointer settled, when, and how fast it is moving. */
  const scrub = {
    x: -1,
    y: -1,
    /** Where the current hold began. */
    ax: -1,
    ay: -1,
    /** Pointer velocity, px/s, for the cat to aim ahead of (§5.3). */
    vx: 0,
    vy: 0,
    at: 0,
    since: 0,
    target: null as HTMLElement | null,
    seen: 0,
    /**
     * Is the pointer even in the window?
     *
     * §5.3: a cursor that leaves mid-leap must be a whiff. Without this the last
     * known position lingers exactly where the cat aimed, so leaving the window
     * counted as standing still and being caught — the opposite of the rule.
     */
    inside: false,
  };

  /**
   * One kitten (§15). A `Prey` that can also walk somewhere and be sat down.
   *
   * It is deliberately the same shape the boss reads off the pointer, plus three fields the
   * pointer has no use for: what it is *walking to* as opposed to holding, whether that came
   * from an order, and when it is allowed to get up again.
   */
  interface Kitten extends Prey {
    /** Its own element — a clone of the cat's drawing, so §0's "nothing new is drawn" holds. */
    el: HTMLElement;
    /** The claim it is on its way to. `target` is the one it has arrived at and is holding. */
    seek: HTMLElement | null;
    /** Flinching from a landed pounce until this moment — visual only (§15). */
    flinchUntil: number;
    /** Which way it is facing, so the drawing flips like the cat's does. */
    facing: number;
  }

  const squad: Kitten[] = [];

  /** What the boss is hunting. Re-pointed once a frame; the pointer in manual mode. */
  let quarry: Prey = scrub;

  /**
   * The current touch, so a **tap** can be told from a **hold** (§3, §5.2 as answered in 1.2).
   *
   * On a mouse these are different events: `click` throws, dwelling scrubs, and nothing about
   * one produces the other. A finger does both with the same gesture — `click` fires when it
   * lifts — so scrubbing a claim would have spent a paw on release, every single time. The
   * discriminator has to be what the touch *did*, not where it was: a tap is brief and reclaimed
   * nothing; anything that made scrub progress was the player working, and working is not asking
   * to throw.
   */
  const touch = {
    /** When the finger went down, and 0 for a mouse — see the `pointerdown` handler. */
    at: 0,
    /**
     * Did this touch ever have a claim under it? Recorded *while it happens*, because a completed
     * hold frees the element before the click arrives — see `isWorking` in `arena.ts`.
     */
    onClaim: false,
  };

  const el = (id: string) => document.getElementById(id);

  /* ---------------------------------------------------------------- *
   * Building the board (§4.1)
   * ---------------------------------------------------------------- */

  /**
   * Everything on this page the cat could take.
   *
   * Queried, filtered, then de-nested — in that order, because `dropNested` is
   * O(n²) and the filters cut the list by about two thirds first.
   */
  function candidates(): HTMLElement[] {
    const all = [...document.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>(CLAIMABLE)];
    const keep = all.filter((node) => {
      if (node.matches(PROTECTED)) return false; // self only — see arena.ts
      if (node.closest(PROTECTED_TREE)) return false; // subtree
      const r = node.getBoundingClientRect();
      return bigEnough(r.width, r.height);
    });
    const nested = dropNested(keep, (outer, inner) => outer.contains(inner));
    // §4: the arena is the viewport plus what it can see. `boardSlice` keeps the fight on
    // the screen it is named after, and borrows from below the fold only if the screen is
    // too thin to hold a game.
    const vh = document.documentElement.clientHeight;
    const inView = nested.map((n) => {
      const r = n.getBoundingClientRect();
      return r.bottom > 0 && r.top < vh;
    });
    // Both modes get §4.1's board. 2.0 briefly dealt commander mode a smaller one to keep the walks
    // short, and that was the wrong layer to fix it at — see `SQUAD_BOARD_MIN`'s retraction.
    return boardSlice(inView).map((i) => nested[i]!);
  }

  /**
   * The `style` attribute each claimed element arrived with, verbatim.
   *
   * Undoing a claim property-by-property is not the same as putting the attribute
   * back: touching an element's style through the CSSOM re-serialises whatever was
   * already inline, so an element that came in with
   * `style="background-image:var(--lqip)"` can come out spelled differently and be
   * *visually* identical while no longer being the page as found. Pillar 2 says the
   * DOM is restored exactly, so hold the string and hand it back.
   */
  const wasStyled = new WeakMap<HTMLElement, string | null>();

  /** Paint a claim on. One custom property; the rest of the look is the class. */
  function claim(node: HTMLElement, index: number, seed: number): void {
    /*
     * Nothing of ours may be in the snapshot. `free()` restores this attribute verbatim, so a
     * `--scrub` left behind by an interrupted hold would be recorded as the element's *original*
     * style and restored on the way out — pillar 2 failing, from a mark nobody removed. 1.4 shipped
     * this exact shape once via the siege sweep; 2.0 has four kittens that can each leave one, so
     * the single choke point cleans up instead of trusting four callers to.
     */
    clearWash(node);
    wasStyled.set(node, node.getAttribute('style'));
    node.style.setProperty('--claim-tilt', `${claimTilt(index, seed).toFixed(3)}deg`);
    node.classList.add('cat-claimed');
  }

  /** Take one back, leaving the element exactly as it was found. */
  function free(node: HTMLElement, celebrate: boolean): void {
    node.classList.remove('cat-claimed');
    const before = wasStyled.get(node) ?? null;
    if (before === null) node.removeAttribute('style');
    else node.setAttribute('style', before);
    wasStyled.delete(node);
    if (!celebrate) return;
    node.classList.add('cat-freed');
    setTimeout(() => node.classList.remove('cat-freed'), 480);
  }

  /* ---------------------------------------------------------------- *
   * The HUD line — borrowed, then given back
   * ---------------------------------------------------------------- */

  function caption(text: string): void {
    arena.caption = text;
    const line = document.querySelector<HTMLElement>('#cat-score .cat-caption');
    if (line && line.textContent !== text) line.textContent = text;
  }

  /**
   * Hold the line the arena borrowed.
   *
   * SiteCat's `announce()` swaps the caption to the level phrase on a find and sets a
   * 3.2s timer to put the tally back. That timer does not know about the fight, so it
   * fired mid-fight and left the caption reading "3 / 7 treats" while the paw row
   * beside it was showing 2 — the row is ammo during a fight, so the two were flatly
   * contradicting each other. Caught in a screenshot, not by a test.
   *
   * Re-asserting once a frame is the cheap fix and it is self-healing: whatever writes
   * to that line, it goes back to the truth on the next tick.
   */
  function holdCaption(): void {
    if (!arena.caption) return;
    caption(arena.caption);
  }

  /**
   * Put the treat tally back exactly as the cat had it.
   *
   * Recomputed rather than remembered: SiteCat owns that state and may well have
   * changed it while the fight was on (arriving on a new tab credits a treat), so
   * a string cached at the start would be stale. The paw row is the source of
   * truth for both numbers, which is the same trick `readTabs` uses.
   */
  function restoreCaption(): void {
    const paws = [...document.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>('#cat-score .cat-paw[data-slug]')];
    if (!paws.length) return;
    caption(tallyFor(paws.filter((p) => p.classList.contains('got')).length, paws.length));
  }

  /**
   * Give the paw row back exactly as it was found.
   *
   * §7.2: losing a fight must never cost you exploration, so treats spent here are
   * spent *inside* the fight only. Must run before `restoreCaption`, which counts the
   * filled paws to rebuild the tally — restoring in the other order publishes the
   * ammo count as if it were the score.
   */
  function restoreAmmo(): void {
    const paws = [...document.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>('#cat-score .cat-paw[data-slug]')];
    paws.forEach((paw, i) => {
      /*
       * The snapshot restores; it never rolls back. `||`, not a plain `toggle` to the
       * snapshot's value — a paw that was filled *during* the fight keeps its fill.
       *
       * Reachable, and it cost the visitor a find. Leaving a page with an uncollected treat
       * credits it (`collect(pending)` in SiteCat, "you were there, which is the thing being
       * rewarded"), and SiteCat is mounted first so its `astro:page-load` handler runs before
       * the one here that ends the fight. So: open a fight on a tab whose treat is still on the
       * floor, click a link, and the row lit the new paw, played the level-up, and then this
       * function took it straight back off — leaving the HUD saying one thing and `game.found`
       * saying another until the next navigation quietly re-synced them.
       *
       * §7.2 is that a fight must never cost you exploration. It says it about *losing*; it
       * meant it about the fight.
       */
      const found = paw.classList.contains('got');
      paw.classList.toggle('got', found || (arena.ammoWas[i] ?? false));
      paw.classList.remove('withheld');
    });
    arena.ammoWas = [];
  }

  /**
   * Take the rung's worth of treats off the table for this fight (§9.4).
   *
   * Runs straight after the snapshot in `open()`, so `restoreAmmo` gives them back on close —
   * the handicap is fight-local for the same reason spending is (§7.2). Nothing else needs to
   * know: `ammo()` counts `.got`, so `isLost`'s ammo term, the throw handler and §8.2's
   * `support-bribe` all pick the cap up for free.
   *
   * **From the right, deterministically.** Two consequences worth being deliberate about.
   * It takes a *specific* treat, not a count, so the ladder narrows your kit as well as
   * thinning it — a rung 2 fight is missing two particular tools from §9.5's loadout, and
   * which ones is a fact about your own exploration. And it is reproducible: the same
   * found-set always yields the same handicapped fight, the bargain §9.2's seed makes for
   * the board.
   *
   * The withheld paws get their own class rather than merely losing `got`, because an unfilled
   * paw already means two things — "not found" outside a fight, "spent" inside one — and a
   * third meaning wearing the same face would make the HUD lie about the one number the
   * visitor is being asked to plan around.
   */
  function withholdForRung(paws: HTMLElement[]): void {
    const filled = paws.filter((p) => p.classList.contains('got'));
    const keep = ammoCap(filled.length, arena.rung);
    for (const paw of filled.slice(keep)) {
      paw.classList.remove('got');
      paw.classList.add('withheld');
    }
  }

  /* ---------------------------------------------------------------- *
   * Scrub loop
   * ---------------------------------------------------------------- */

  const ring = () => el('cat-scrub');

  function showRing(x: number, y: number, progress: number): void {
    const r = ring();
    if (!r) return;
    r.hidden = false;
    r.style.transform = `translate(${x}px, ${y}px)`;
    const fill = r.querySelector<SVGCircleElement>('.scrub-fill');
    // 106.81 = 2πr, matching the dasharray in the stylesheet
    fill?.style.setProperty('stroke-dashoffset', String(106.81 * (1 - progress)));
  }

  function hideRing(): void {
    const r = ring();
    if (r) r.hidden = true;
  }

  /**
   * Claims currently showing hold progress.
   *
   * A single `scrubbing` element until 2.0, because a visitor has one pointer and can only be
   * holding one thing. A squad has four kittens holding four things, so this is a set — and the
   * discipline it was written with matters more now, not less: `free()` restores the `style`
   * attribute verbatim and `claim()` snapshots it, so a `--scrub` left on an element that gets
   * re-claimed is recorded *as the original* and restored on exit. That is pillar 2 failing, and
   * 1.4 already shipped one bug of exactly that shape. With four workers there are four ways to
   * leave a mark behind, so `claim()` now clears one on the way past rather than trusting anybody.
   */
  const washed = new Set<HTMLElement>();

  /** Take the progress mark off one claim. */
  function clearWash(node: HTMLElement): void {
    node.style.removeProperty('--scrub');
    washed.delete(node);
  }

  /** Take it off all of them — the fight is over, or the board is being dealt again. */
  function clearAllWash(): void {
    for (const node of [...washed]) clearWash(node);
  }

  /**
   * Give up a hold, and take its mark with it.
   *
   * **The invariant this exists to make unbreakable:** a progress mark belongs to whoever is
   * holding, so abandoning the hold has to remove it. Until 2.0 that was free, because `hideRing`
   * cleared the single `scrubbing` element and every abandon path happened to call it. With a mark
   * per worker that no longer follows, and `touch-fight` found it immediately: the `pointerout`
   * handler nulls `scrub.target` on a finger lift, so the mark on the claim it had been holding was
   * orphaned — one element still wearing `--scrub` after the finger left, which `claim()` would
   * later snapshot as its original style. Pillar 2, failing by omission.
   *
   * So there is one way to let go of a claim, and it is this. Five callers, none of which has to
   * remember anything.
   */
  function dropHold(hand: Prey): void {
    if (hand.target) clearWash(hand.target);
    hand.target = null;
  }

  /**
   * Where the hold's progress is shown — and on touch it cannot be at the pointer.
   *
   * §5.2 rejected touch partly because "a finger covers the thing it is holding", and it is
   * right: the ring is a 40px circle centred on the contact point, which is the one place a
   * fingertip is guaranteed to hide. So on a coarse pointer the progress goes **into the claim**
   * — it already carries a wash and a dashed edge (§5.1), so it is already the object the player
   * is watching, and filling it needs no new art (§0).
   *
   * One claim at a time, tracked in `scrubbing`, because a stale `--scrub` left on an element the
   * finger has moved off would show a hold that is not happening — and `free()` restores the
   * `style` attribute verbatim, so a custom property left behind would be restored *as the
   * original*, which is pillar 2's exact failure.
   */
  function showScrub(target: HTMLElement, x: number, y: number, progress: number): void {
    /*
     * Commander mode always fills the claim, whatever the pointer is.
     *
     * There is one ring and there can be four kittens, so a ring cannot represent a squad — but
     * the deeper reason is that it should not try. In manual mode the ring is at the pointer
     * because the hold *is* the pointer; in commander mode the visitor is watching the page come
     * back, and the honest place for "this paragraph is being rescued" is the paragraph. It also
     * means 1.2's contrast work on the in-claim wash (§5.1, 7% → 26%) is the thing carrying the
     * whole mode's feedback, which it was measured to be able to do.
     */
    if (!coarse() && !commanding()) {
      showRing(x, y, progress);
      return;
    }
    washed.add(target);
    target.style.setProperty('--scrub', progress.toFixed(3));
  }

  /**
   * Does this visitor's pointer land rather than travel?
   *
   * The query object is made **once** and its `.matches` read per frame, not `matchMedia()` called
   * per frame: `showScrub` runs inside the rAF loop, and minting a fresh `MediaQueryList` sixty
   * times a second to ask a question whose answer almost never changes is the kind of waste §14's
   * frame budget was rewritten to avoid. Still live — a tablet that gains a keyboard case, or a
   * desktop the visitor starts touching, flips `.matches` on the same object.
   */
  const finePointer = matchMedia('(hover: hover) and (pointer: fine)');
  const coarse = () => !finePointer.matches;

  /** The claimed element under the pointer, if any. */
  function claimUnder(x: number, y: number): HTMLElement | null {
    const hit = document.elementFromPoint(x, y);
    if (!(hit instanceof Element)) return null;
    const node = hit.closest<HTMLElement>('.cat-claimed');
    return node && arena.claimed.includes(node) ? node : null;
  }

  /**
   * One frame of *somebody* working a claim — §5.2's verb, performed by whoever is asked (§15).
   *
   * Extracted from `stepScrub` in 2.0 and otherwise unchanged, which is the point: commander mode
   * does not reimplement the core verb, it hands it to a kitten. Every measurement behind
   * `SCRUB_MS`, the drift rule, the reward beat and the win check therefore still describes both
   * modes, and a kitten's hold is the same 1400ms of stillness a visitor's was.
   *
   * `drift` is the one genuine difference between a hand and a kitten, and it matters more than it
   * looks. For a pointer, movement is *intent* — you moved off, so the hold restarts (§5.2). For a
   * kitten, the coordinates move whenever the **page** does, because claims are in document flow
   * and a kitten is fixed to the viewport like the cat. Applying the drift rule to it would mean
   * that scrolling — that is, reading — silently cancelled the squad's progress, and the whole
   * promise of the mode is that reading is what the visitor should be doing.
   */
  function stepHold(
    hand: Prey,
    target: HTMLElement | null,
    now: number,
    opts: { drift: boolean; over?: number },
  ): number {
    if (!target || target !== hand.target) {
      // moved onto something else, or off the board: the hold restarts
      if (hand.target) clearWash(hand.target);
      hand.target = target;
      hand.since = now;
      hand.ax = hand.x;
      hand.ay = hand.y;
      if (!target) hideRing();
      return 0;
    }
    if (opts.drift && !stillEnough(hand.x - hand.ax, hand.y - hand.ay)) {
      // drifted: same target, fresh hold, and the anchor follows the pointer
      hand.since = now;
      hand.ax = hand.x;
      hand.ay = hand.y;
    }
    const progress = scrubProgress(now - hand.since, opts.over);
    showScrub(target, hand.x, hand.y, progress);
    if (progress < 1) return progress;

    // reclaimed
    arena.claimed = arena.claimed.filter((n) => n !== target);
    arena.freed.push(target);
    arena.interrupts = 0;
    arena.movedAt = now;
    // 1.3: taking a claim back is the fight's reward beat, and it has a visual (the wash
    // draining) — so it gets the reward sound.
    playCue('reclaim');
    clearWash(target);
    free(target, true);
    caption(arenaCaption(arena.claimed.length, arena.total));
    paintTerritory();
    hand.target = null;
    hand.since = now;
    hideRing();
    /*
     * Who decides what a cleared board means — and it is the one branch the two modes cannot
     * share. Manual mode wins a fight and moves a rung (§9.4). Commander mode clears a *round*
     * and deals the next one, harder, with one more kitten (§15). Same emptied board, two
     * different things to do about it.
     */
    if (isWon(arena.claimed.length, arena.order.length)) {
      if (commanding()) clearRound(now);
      else finish('win', now);
    }
    return 0;
  }

  /**
   * One frame of the *visitor* scrubbing. Returns progress 0..1, which the boss reads to decide
   * whether you are committed enough to be worth interrupting.
   *
   * Manual mode only. In commander mode the pointer does not work claims at all — it points at
   * them (§15's orders), and a cursor resting on a paragraph is somebody reading rather than
   * somebody playing.
   */
  function stepScrub(now: number): number {
    // The other half of freezing the ending (see the pounce commitment in `stepBoss`): a hold
    // started or continued during the beat wins nothing, because there is nothing left to win.
    // Without this the win beat's one reclaimed element could be scrubbed straight back off
    // under the line announcing it, and the parting shot would be about a thing not on screen.
    if (arena.ending || commanding()) {
      dropHold(scrub);
      hideRing();
      return 0;
    }
    const target = scrub.inside && scrub.x >= 0 ? claimUnder(scrub.x, scrub.y) : null;
    if (target && target === scrub.target) {
      /*
       * Holding a claim **is** playing, and the idle truce has to know that.
       *
       * `scrub.seen` was refreshed only by `pointermove`, and §11's auto-truce ends a fight
       * `IDLE_TRUCE_MS` after the last one. But the core verb of this game (§5.2) is holding the
       * pointer *still*, and 1.0 measured flee-and-hold as the counter the whole fight is built
       * on — so the better you play, the less you move. A player pinned on one claim by a cat
       * that keeps interrupting makes progress, never moves the mouse, and at twenty seconds the
       * game quietly ends itself under them.
       *
       * Found by a harness reporting an emptied board with no win: a truce and a victory look
       * identical from outside, which is why this survived four versions of browser testing.
       */
      scrub.seen = now;
      /*
       * Remembered here because this is the only place that knows a hold had a claim under it —
       * and it has to be remembered rather than re-derived, since a *completed* hold frees the
       * element before the click that ends the touch arrives. `isWorking` is what reads it.
       */
      touch.onClaim = true;
    }
    return stepHold(scrub, target, now, { drift: true });
  }

  /* ---------------------------------------------------------------- *
   * The squad (§15) — kittens, which do the holding now
   * ---------------------------------------------------------------- */

  const squadEl = () => el('cat-squad');

  /**
   * Make a kitten out of the cat that is already on the page.
   *
   * **`cloneNode`, not `createElement`, and that is the whole trick.** §0 forbids new art, and
   * `SiteCat.astro` writes down why hand-built elements do not work here: Astro scopes a
   * component's styles to the elements *it* rendered, so a `createElement`'d paw "never matched
   * `.cat-paw` and came out 0x0 and invisible". A clone carries the scope class in its `class`
   * attribute, so it keeps the cat's own drawing *and* the base rules for it — and the kitten is
   * then literally the same animal, smaller, which is exactly the fiction wanted.
   *
   * Returns null before the cat exists (a page without `SiteCat`), which the caller treats as "no
   * squad this round" rather than as an error: the arena is decoration and must never be the
   * reason a page fails.
   */
  function makeKitten(now: number): Kitten | null {
    const flip = document.querySelector<HTMLElement>('#site-cat .cat-flip');
    const box = squadEl();
    if (!flip || !box) return null;
    const node = document.createElement('div');
    node.className = 'cat-kit';
    node.appendChild(flip.cloneNode(true));
    box.appendChild(node);
    // Starts where the cat is, because that is where kittens come from — and it means the squad
    // arrives from the same corner the boss just left rather than appearing mid-page.
    const from = catEl()?.getBoundingClientRect();
    return {
      el: node,
      x: from ? from.left + from.width / 2 : 40,
      y: from ? from.top + from.height / 2 : window.innerHeight - 40,
      ax: 0,
      ay: 0,
      vx: 0,
      vy: 0,
      at: now,
      since: now,
      inside: true,
      target: null,
      seek: null,
      flinchUntil: 0,
      facing: 1,
    };
  }

  /**
   * Put the kitten where its numbers say it is, and publish what it is doing.
   *
   * The two `data-` attributes are for the same reason `data-mood` and `data-stance` exist on the
   * boss: this is real fight state, it is worth being able to see from outside the closure, and a
   * browser harness that can only see pixels ends up asserting on pixels — which §12 has recorded
   * as the way a check passes while the mechanic is broken. `data-hold` is *which claim*, not how
   * far along, because progress is already visible as the wash on the claim itself.
   */
  function placeKitten(k: Kitten): void {
    k.el.style.transform = `translate(${Math.round(k.x)}px, ${Math.round(k.y)}px) scale(${k.facing * 0.62}, 0.62)`;
    const seek = k.seek ? arena.order.indexOf(k.seek) : -1;
    const hold = k.target ? arena.order.indexOf(k.target) : -1;
    k.el.dataset.seek = String(seek);
    k.el.dataset.hold = String(hold);
  }

  /** The squad this round is fought with, dealt fresh so a round cannot inherit a stale animal. */
  function dealSquad(now: number): void {
    disbandSquad();
    const want = kittensFor(arena.round);
    for (let i = 0; i < want; i++) {
      const k = makeKitten(now);
      if (!k) break;
      // Fanned out along the floor, so two kittens do not start life inside each other.
      k.x += i * 22;
      squad.push(k);
      placeKitten(k);
    }
  }

  /** Take them off the page, and their marks with them. */
  function disbandSquad(): void {
    for (const k of squad) {
      if (k.target) clearWash(k.target);
      k.el.remove();
    }
    squad.length = 0;
  }

  /** Where a claim is, in the space the fight happens in. */
  function centreOf(node: HTMLElement): { x: number; y: number } {
    const r = node.getBoundingClientRect();
    return { x: r.left + r.width / 2, y: r.top + r.height / 2 };
  }

  /**
   * The board as the policy sees it: every claim still standing, with a viewport position.
   *
   * Viewport space, like the cat and the kittens, because the fight is fought in it — the boss is
   * `position: fixed` and so is a kitten, and mixing document and viewport coordinates is the
   * mistake §12 recorded twice ("measure in the space the mechanic lives in"). A consequence worth
   * being clear about: a claim scrolled far off screen has an off-screen position, so a kitten will
   * walk off the visible page to reach it. That is correct — the work is still happening, the HUD
   * still counts it — and it is what lets a visitor scroll away without stalling the round.
   */
  function boardCandidates(): Candidate[] {
    const out: Candidate[] = [];
    for (const node of arena.claimed) {
      if (!node.isConnected) continue;
      const c = centreOf(node);
      out.push({ index: arena.order.indexOf(node), x: c.x, y: c.y });
    }
    return out;
  }

  /**
   * One frame of the whole squad (§15).
   *
   * Each kitten does one of two things and never anything else: it walks to the claim it means to
   * work, or it works it. (It also flinches when hit, which is paint rather than behaviour — see
   * `KITTEN_FLINCH_MS`.) The intelligence is entirely in `pickWork`, which
   * is `arena.ts`-style pure and which implements the strategy the browser harnesses proved wins —
   * so what happens here is only the moving.
   */
  function stepSquad(now: number, dt: number): void {
    if (!commanding() || arena.ending) return;
    const at = { x: boss.x, y: boss.y };
    const candidates = boardCandidates();
    const taken = squad.map((k) => (k.seek ? arena.order.indexOf(k.seek) : -1));

    for (const k of squad) {
      /*
       * The flinch is a *reaction shot* and nothing else — it does not stop the kitten (§15).
       *
       * Two earlier versions blocked here, and both made the mode unwinnable: 0.7's measurement is
       * that a stationary worker beats the cat's pounce cycle by about twenty milliseconds, so any
       * pause at all loses every exchange. See `KITTEN_FLINCH_MS` for the whole arithmetic. What a
       * hit costs is the hold, which `land` has already taken.
       */
      k.el.classList.toggle('kit-hit', now < k.flinchUntil);

      // Has what it was going for gone? Somebody else may have freed it, or the cat re-took it.
      if (k.seek && (!k.seek.isConnected || !arena.claimed.includes(k.seek))) {
        k.seek = null;
      }
      /*
       * Pick, or **re-pick when the trip stopped being worth making**.
       *
       * A kitten keeps its errand: `seek` survives until the claim is freed or taken back, which is
       * what makes an order stick and what stops it dithering. The one exception is the case that
       * made this necessary — when nothing on the board can be finished, `pickWork` falls back to
       * walking *away* from the cat, and that walk is only ever a means to an end. Committing to it
       * blindly is how a kitten ends up marching across a document while a claim it could have
       * finished sits beside it. So: if what it is walking to cannot be finished from here, and
       * something else can, it changes its mind.
       */
      const slot = squad.indexOf(k);
      const seekIndex = k.seek ? arena.order.indexOf(k.seek) : -1;
      const stillWorth =
        k.seek !== null &&
        (k.target === k.seek ||
          candidates.some((c) => c.index === seekIndex && canFinish(k, c, at)));
      if (!stillWorth) {
        const mine = pickWork(
          { x: k.x, y: k.y, target: seekIndex >= 0 ? seekIndex : null },
          candidates,
          at,
          taken.filter((t) => t >= 0),
        );
        const chosen = mine >= 0 ? (arena.order[mine] ?? null) : null;
        // Only switch to something *better*: a fallback pick that lands on a different unworkable
        // claim every frame would be dithering, which looks broken and reclaims nothing.
        if (chosen !== k.seek) {
          const worth = candidates.some((c) => c.index === mine && canFinish(k, c, at));
          if (!k.seek || worth) {
            dropHold(k);
            k.seek = chosen;
            taken[slot] = mine;
          }
        }
      }
      if (!k.seek) {
        // Nothing left to take — the round is ending, or every claim is somebody else's.
        k.el.classList.remove('walking');
        stepHold(k, null, now, { drift: false, over: KITTEN_WORK_MS });
        placeKitten(k);
        continue;
      }

      const to = centreOf(k.seek);
      const dx = to.x - k.x;
      const dy = to.y - k.y;
      const away = Math.hypot(dx, dy);
      /*
       * **Arrival is sticky, and it has to be.** The test is `away > ARRIVE_PX` only while the
       * kitten is *not already holding this claim*; once it is holding, it stays holding until the
       * claim is gone. Without that, a claim whose measured centre drifts a pixel across the
       * threshold — and a `.cat-claimed` element is tilted, so its bounding box is a rotated
       * rectangle that moves under any reflow — flips the kitten between "walk" and "hold" on
       * alternate frames. Each flip hands `stepHold` a different target, which restarts the hold,
       * which means it never completes. Measured: a kitten sat at one spot for fifteen seconds with
       * the wash showing and nothing ever coming back.
       */
      const holding = k.target === k.seek;
      if (!holding && away > ARRIVE_PX) {
        const step = Math.min(away, KITTEN_SPEED * dt);
        k.x += (dx / away) * step;
        k.y += (dy / away) * step;
        k.vx = (dx / away) * KITTEN_SPEED;
        k.vy = (dy / away) * KITTEN_SPEED;
        k.at = now;
        if (Math.abs(dx) > 2) k.facing = dx < 0 ? -1 : 1;
        k.el.classList.add('walking');
        // Walking is not working: a hold only exists once it has arrived, which is what makes
        // distance cost the squad something and therefore what makes `pickWork`'s choice matter.
        stepHold(k, null, now, { drift: false, over: KITTEN_WORK_MS });
        placeKitten(k);
        continue;
      }

      k.vx = 0;
      k.vy = 0;
      k.el.classList.remove('walking');
      stepHold(k, k.seek, now, { drift: false, over: KITTEN_WORK_MS });
      placeKitten(k);
    }
  }

  /**
   * §15's order: send somebody to that claim.
   *
   * The commander's whole vocabulary, and it is one line of state — the kitten's `seek`. Everything
   * that makes it *work* is already here: the kitten walks with the same code it always walks with,
   * holds with the same code any hand holds with. Nothing needs to mark it as *ordered*, because
   * `stepSquad` only ever re-picks for a kitten with no `seek` at all — so an instruction survives
   * by construction until the claim is freed or taken back, and a bad order is honoured like any
   * other. That is the right behaviour: a commander is allowed to be wrong.
   *
   * An order is a suggestion about *what*, never about how or when. There is no cancel, no queue and
   * no cost, because each of those would turn a nudge into an interface — and the mode is for
   * somebody who may not have realised there is a game on the page.
   */
  function order(claim: HTMLElement, x: number, y: number): void {
    if (!arena.claimed.includes(claim)) return;
    const who = assignOrder(
      squad.map((k) => ({ x: k.x, y: k.y, target: k.seek ? arena.order.indexOf(k.seek) : null })),
      { x, y },
    );
    const k = who >= 0 ? squad[who] : undefined;
    if (!k) return;
    // Whatever it was holding stops being held — including its wash, or the abandoned claim keeps a
    // half-full mark that `claim()` would later snapshot as its original style (pillar 2).
    dropHold(k);
    k.seek = claim;
    playCue('order');
    k.el.classList.add('kit-called');
    window.setTimeout(() => k.el.classList.remove('kit-called'), 360);
  }

  /**
   * Which kitten the boss is hunting: the nearest one (§15).
   *
   * Nearest rather than weakest or busiest, because that is the version a watching visitor can
   * predict — the threat is always the animal closest to the cat, which is a thing you can see
   * without being told. Startled kittens stay eligible: a cat looming over a sitting kitten is
   * both correct and the clearest possible picture of what a hit costs.
   */
  function nearestKitten(): Kitten | null {
    let best: Kitten | null = null;
    let bestD = Infinity;
    for (const k of squad) {
      const d = Math.hypot(k.x - boss.x, k.y - boss.y);
      if (d < bestD) {
        bestD = d;
        best = k;
      }
    }
    return best;
  }

  function tick(now: number): void {
    if (!arena.on) return;
    arena.raf = requestAnimationFrame(tick);

    /*
     * auto-truce: a fight nobody is playing is over (§11) — and 2.0 has to ask that question
     * differently, because in commander mode *watching* is the intended way to play.
     *
     * Manual mode keeps the 20s clock exactly as 1.1 corrected it. Commander mode uses the longer
     * `WATCH_TRUCE_MS` and measures presence rather than play: any move, scroll or keypress
     * refreshes it, so a reader is present and a three-minute silence means the tab was left open.
     * With the 20s clock the mode's central experience would end itself twice a minute.
     */
    const patience = commanding() ? WATCH_TRUCE_MS : IDLE_TRUCE_MS;
    if (scrub.seen && now - scrub.seen > patience) {
      endQuietly();
      return;
    }

    // Clamped, like the ink wash clamps its own: a tab that comes back from the
    // background hands you a multi-second frame, and an unclamped stalk would
    // teleport the cat onto the cursor and pounce from nowhere.
    const dt = Math.min(0.05, (now - boss.last) / 1000);
    boss.last = now;

    // Treat first: a landing has to be visible to the boss in the same frame, or the
    // cat spends a frame stalking something it should already have abandoned.
    stepTreat(now);
    // Then the mood, before anything reads it: the tier decides how fast the cat winds
    // up, how soon it commits, how quickly it walks and how often it speaks, so a
    // stale one for a frame means four things disagreeing about the same fight.
    stepMood(now);
    /*
     * The squad moves before the boss, and then the boss is pointed at it (§15).
     *
     * Order matters for the same reason the treat goes first: the cat must stalk where its quarry
     * *is* this frame, not where it was. And `quarry` is re-pointed once, here, so everything below
     * — the pounce, the aim prediction, the anchor leash, 1.4's swat — reads one consistent answer
     * to "who am I hunting" for the whole frame.
     */
    stepSquad(now, dt);
    const worked = stepScrub(now);
    quarry = commanding() ? (nearestKitten() ?? scrub) : scrub;
    stepBoss(now, dt, commanding() ? scrubProgress(now - quarry.since) * (quarry.target ? 1 : 0) : worked);
    holdCaption();
    // Nothing talks while an ending is playing: the ending has the last word.
    if (!arena.ending) stepTalk(now);
  }

  /* ---------------------------------------------------------------- *
   * The boss (§5.3) — stalk, telegraph, leap, recover
   * ---------------------------------------------------------------- */

  /** The cat, in client coordinates, as a thing with one phase at a time. */
  const boss = {
    x: 0,
    y: 0,
    phase: 'stalk' as Phase,
    /** When the current phase began. */
    since: 0,
    /** Leap endpoints, locked when the telegraph ends. */
    ax: 0,
    ay: 0,
    bx: 0,
    by: 0,
    facing: 1 as 1 | -1,
    last: 0,
    /** This fight's stance (§9.3) — same verbs, different opponent. */
    stance: 'ambush' as Stance,
    /** True when the current wind-up is a bluff (trickster). */
    feint: false,
    /** A bell makes the *next* telegraph readable; consumed when it fires. */
    telegraphMul: 1,
    /** Yarn tangles its feet: slowed until this timestamp. */
    slowUntil: 0,
    slowMul: 1,
    /** Feather and biscuit pin it near a spot: it will not stray past this. */
    anchorX: 0,
    anchorY: 0,
    anchorPx: 0,
    anchorUntil: 0,
    /**
     * Raised for one tick when the desperate tier is entered, so §8 can announce the last stand.
     * Consumed by `fightState`; see the note on `FightState.lastStand`.
     */
    lastStand: false,
    /** Siege regrows the board on this clock. */
    regrowAt: 0,
    /**
     * The swat pose's own timer, so a stale one cannot un-pose the *next* swat.
     *
     * Held rather than fired and forgotten because 1.1's review found four bugs of exactly this
     * shape — a timer outliving the thing it was scheduled for — and the ending beat's was one
     * of them. A swat late in one fight and another early in the next is the reachable case.
     */
    swatTimer: 0,
    /**
     * Regrow ticks so far this fight, so §9.3's siege sweep can land on a *beat*.
     *
     * Counted rather than randomised: a beat is the thing a player can learn to anticipate,
     * and 1.4's whole argument for the sweep is that a random second claim would read as the
     * clock ticking twice. Reset with the fight, like every other boss field.
     */
    regrows: 0,
    /** How long the cat cannot pounce while at a treat, from the treat's spec. */
    immuneMs: 0,
    lureMs: 0,
    /**
     * How the fight is going, as the cat reads it (§7.3).
     *
     * Held rather than recomputed from scratch each frame because `mood` is hysteretic:
     * which tier you are in depends on which tier you were in, and a boundary with no
     * memory strobes on alternate exchanges.
     */
    mood: 'even' as Mood,
    /** When the bored cat last washed, and until when it is busy doing so. */
    groomedAt: 0,
    groomUntil: 0,
  };

  const catEl = () => el('site-cat');

  /** Half the cat, measured rather than hardcoded — SiteCat owns its own size. */
  function catHalf(root: HTMLElement): { w: number; h: number } {
    return { w: root.offsetWidth / 2, h: root.offsetHeight / 2 };
  }

  /**
   * Put the cat's centre at a client point.
   *
   * `#site-cat` is pinned `left: 0; bottom: 0`, so its untransformed centre sits at
   * (halfW, viewportH - halfH) and everything is an offset from there. Writing the
   * transform is the only thing this component does to the element itself.
   */
  function placeCat(root: HTMLElement, cx: number, cy: number): void {
    const { w, h } = catHalf(root);
    const vw = document.documentElement.clientWidth;
    const vh = document.documentElement.clientHeight;
    const x = Math.max(w, Math.min(vw - w, cx));
    const y = Math.max(h, Math.min(vh - h, cy));
    boss.x = x;
    boss.y = y;
    root.style.transform = `translate(${(x - w).toFixed(1)}px, ${(y - vh + h).toFixed(1)}px)`;
  }

  function bossPhase(root: HTMLElement, phase: Phase, now: number): void {
    boss.phase = phase;
    boss.since = now;
    // 1.3: the wind-up sounds, because it has a visual — the telegraph is the one cue
    // a player needs to hear to feel the pounce coming. A feint is a telegraph too
    // (it gathers, then thinks better), so it sounds the same.
    if (phase === 'telegraph') playCue('telegraph');
    root.classList.toggle('boss-telegraph', phase === 'telegraph');
    root.classList.toggle('boss-leap', phase === 'leap');
    root.classList.toggle('boss-recover', phase === 'recover');
    root.classList.toggle('boss-eat', phase === 'eat');
    root.classList.toggle('boss-fetch', phase === 'fetch');
    // §9.3 says the trickster's feint can be learned, which means there has to be a tell.
    // This is it: a bluff gathers without dropping its shoulders.
    root.classList.toggle('boss-feint', phase === 'telegraph' && boss.feint);
    /*
     * `walking` is SiteCat's own class, and its leg and bob keyframes are already
     * written. The boss was moving with its legs still: the arena parks SiteCat's
     * loop, and that loop is what used to set this. Reusing it means the two phases
     * that actually travel animate, and nothing new is drawn.
     */
    root.classList.toggle('walking', phase === 'stalk' || phase === 'fetch');
  }

  /**
   * Stop washing, whatever the reason.
   *
   * Its own function because it is called from four places — the wash finishing, the mood
   * lifting mid-wash, handing the cat back, and closing the fight — and because the class
   * belongs to `SiteCat`. Leaving it set would hand the ambient cat a grooming class it
   * never asked for; `idleBusiness()` would eventually clear it, but only once the cat
   * next goes idle, which is not a guarantee worth relying on for somebody else's element.
   */
  function endGrooming(root: HTMLElement | null): void {
    boss.groomUntil = 0;
    root?.classList.remove('grooming');
  }

  /** Turn to face the way it is going. `.face-left` is SiteCat's own class. */
  function faceToward(root: HTMLElement, dx: number): void {
    const face = dx < 0 ? -1 : 1;
    if (face === boss.facing) return;
    boss.facing = face as 1 | -1;
    root.classList.toggle('face-left', face === -1);
  }

  /* ---------------------------------------------------------------- *
   * Treats (§5.4) — exploration, spent
   * ---------------------------------------------------------------- */

  const throwEl = () => el('cat-throw');

  /**
   * Ammunition: the filled paws in the HUD row.
   *
   * Pillar 5 — the fight is a *sink* for the treat economy the site already has, not
   * a new one. So a visitor who has browsed nothing has nothing to throw, and the tab
   * bar is the tutorial. §5.4 calls that state normal rather than an error: the cat
   * just looks at you, and silence is the feedback.
   */
  function ammo(): HTMLElement[] {
    return [...document.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>('#cat-score .cat-paw.got')];
  }

  /** Put the treat somewhere, in the same coordinates the cat uses. */
  function placeThrow(node: HTMLElement, x: number, y: number): void {
    const vh = document.documentElement.clientHeight;
    const t = `translate(${x.toFixed(1)}px, ${(y - vh).toFixed(1)}px)`;
    // the custom property lets the "being eaten" keyframe scale *about* this spot
    // instead of throwing the treat back to the corner to shrink
    node.style.setProperty('--throw-at', t);
    node.style.transform = t;
  }

  /**
   * Spend one treat at the pointer.
   *
   * Three ways this does nothing, all silent and all costing nothing: no ammo, a treat
   * already on the board, or the arena not running. §5.4 asks for silence on the first
   * — an error state for touching a game you opted into would be absurd — and the
   * second is what stops lures being banked now that a badly timed throw is no longer
   * "wasted" (see the note in the GDD's 0.5 entry).
   */
  function throwTreat(x: number, y: number): void {
    // A fourth way, added in 1.1: not once the fight is decided. The ending is a beat rather
    // than a stop, so `arena.on` is still true for a couple of seconds after a win or a loss —
    // and a treat arcing across the screen under the parting line, spending a paw on a fight
    // that is over, is the same "nothing new during the ending" rule the pounce and the regrow
    // now keep. Silent, like the other three.
    if (!arena.on || arena.ending || arena.treat) return;
    const paws = ammo();
    const paw = paws[paws.length - 1];
    if (!paw) return;
    const node = throwEl();
    if (!node) return;

    // The shape is the shape of the treat being spent — the behaviour is one type for
    // every throw (step 3 is "a single type"), but the row shows what you used, which
    // is what §9.5's loadout will eventually make matter.
    const kind = resolveTreat(paw.dataset.slug ?? '');
    node.querySelector('use')?.setAttribute('href', `#treat-${kind}`);
    paw.classList.remove('got');

    // thrown from the stash, which is the widget the paws live in
    const hud = el('cat-hud')?.getBoundingClientRect();
    const from = hud
      ? { x: hud.left + hud.width / 2, y: hud.top + hud.height / 2 }
      : { x: 20, y: document.documentElement.clientHeight - 20 };
    arena.treat = { x, y, fx: from.x, fy: from.y, at: performance.now(), landed: false, kind };
    arena.spent++;
    node.classList.remove('eaten');
    node.removeAttribute('hidden');
    placeThrow(node, from.x, from.y);
  }

  /** One frame of a treat in the air. */
  function stepTreat(now: number): void {
    const t = arena.treat;
    const node = throwEl();
    if (!t || !node || t.landed) return;
    const k = (now - t.at) / THROW_ARC_MS;
    if (k >= 1) {
      t.landed = true;
      placeThrow(node, t.x, t.y);
      /*
       * §5.4's counter (1.4): a treat that lands *on a recovering cat* is a swat.
       *
       * Not a fourth verb — §3 is proud of having three and rejects inputs that add no decision.
       * This is a second thing the throw already in the game can do, and it adds the decision §3
       * asks for: spend the treat to make your next window safe (the lure, since 0.5), or spend
       * it to punish a whiff you just read. Same button, same resource, two intentions.
       *
       * Resolved on *landing* rather than on the click, which is what makes it a skill window
       * rather than a spell: the treat takes `THROW_ARC_MS` to arrive, so you are aiming at where
       * the cat will still be, during a recovery you had to see coming. `swatLands` holds the
       * rule (recover-only, and a radius) in `arena.ts` where it is testable without a DOM.
       *
       * Nothing teaches this. §7.4 wants one mechanic found by exploration, and the tell §5.3
       * already trained you to read is the same tell that marks the punishable moment — so the
       * knowledge the game already gave you is what unlocks it.
       */
      const catRoot = catEl();
      if (catRoot && !arena.ending) {
        const dist = Math.hypot(t.x - boss.x, t.y - boss.y);
        if (swatLands(boss.phase, dist)) {
          /*
           * Extends the recovery it interrupted rather than starting a new state: the FSM measures
           * `now - boss.since` against the phase duration, so moving `since` *is* the stun, and the
           * cat cannot telegraph out of a recovery it has not finished.
           *
           * **One line, and the first version of it quietly meant something else.**
           * `now + SWAT_STUN_MS - RECOVER_MS` reads like "make this recovery `SWAT_STUN_MS` long",
           * but the FSM measures against `RECOVER_MS * spec.recover` — so what it did was *restart*
           * the stance's whole recovery and add 200ms, and one treat then bought 580ms against
           * siege and 895ms against ambush. Nobody designed that: it is a §9.3 multiplier leaking
           * into a §5.4 price, and §10 is supposed to be the only place a number like that is set.
           *
           * `+=` is the version `SWAT_STUN_MS`'s own derivation already describes — "900ms *added*
           * to a recovery", 700 + 900 against a 1400ms hold. It is also the version that is uniform
           * in the quantity the player actually feels, which is not how long the cat is down but
           * **how much longer than it would have been**. And it cannot shorten a recovery by
           * construction, so the one way a punish could accidentally reward the cat is closed
           * without a clamp to get wrong.
           */
          boss.since += SWAT_STUN_MS;
          catRoot.classList.add('boss-swatted');
          clearTimeout(boss.swatTimer);
          boss.swatTimer = window.setTimeout(
            () => catRoot.classList.remove('boss-swatted'),
            Math.min(SWAT_STUN_MS, 1200),
          );
          playCue('swat');
        }
      }
      return;
    }
    // reuse the leap's arc: same hop, thrown rather than jumped
    const p = leapPos(t.fx, t.fy, t.x, t.y, k);
    placeThrow(node, p.x, p.y);
  }

  /** The cat has finished with it. */
  function finishEating(root: HTMLElement, now: number): void {
    const node = throwEl();
    node?.setAttribute('hidden', '');
    node?.classList.remove('eaten');
    arena.treat = null;
    bossPhase(root, 'stalk', now);
  }

  /** Wipe any treat off the board without the cat having eaten it (truce). */
  function clearTreat(): void {
    const node = throwEl();
    node?.setAttribute('hidden', '');
    node?.classList.remove('eaten');
    arena.treat = null;
  }

  /* ---------------------------------------------------------------- *
   * Territory (§6) and dialogue (§8)
   * ---------------------------------------------------------------- */

  /** Paint the bar. Player's share, so it grows as you win. */
  function paintTerritory(arrive = false): void {
    const bar = el('cat-territory');
    if (!bar) return;
    const mine = 1 - territory(arena.claimed.length, arena.order.length);
    // 1.3: the boss grows with the cat's share of the page, so the escalation is readable
    // without a number. The scale is published as a CSS variable on the cat element and
    // consumed by the `.boss` rules in the style block above.
    const share = territory(arena.claimed.length, arena.order.length);
    catEl()?.style.setProperty('--boss-scale', String(1.2 + share * 0.6));
    bar.hidden = false;
    const fill = bar.querySelector<HTMLElement>('.territory-mine');
    if (!fill) return;
    const width = `${(mine * 100).toFixed(1)}%`;
    if (!arrive) {
      fill.style.transitionDelay = '';
      fill.style.width = width;
      return;
    }
    /*
     * §14.3 wants the bar to *arrive* rather than to be found already there, and to arrive
     * from an edge, because direction is meaning. A 3px strip cannot slide anywhere legibly,
     * so what arrives is the **fill**, measured out from the left while the ink dries off it.
     *
     * Two problems, both about timing rather than looks. A width transition on an element
     * that was `display: none` one statement ago has nothing to transition *from*, hence the
     * double rAF. And `open()` runs during the hold, with the screen opaque — so a sweep
     * starting immediately would happen entirely behind the curtain.
     *
     * The delay is `HOLD_FLOOR_MS + 55% of the reveal`, not merely the hold, and the extra is
     * measured rather than taste: drying is a smoothstep, so the sheet is still at 0.99 cover
     * a fifth of the way into the reveal and only opens up past the halfway mark. Waiting for
     * the hold alone put one growth frame in six where it could be seen.
     */
    fill.style.width = '0%';
    fill.style.transitionDelay = `${Math.round(HOLD_FLOOR_MS + REVEAL_MS * 0.55)}ms`;
    requestAnimationFrame(() => {
      requestAnimationFrame(() => {
        fill.style.width = width;
      });
    });
  }

  function hideTerritory(): void {
    const bar = el('cat-territory');
    if (!bar) return;
    bar.hidden = true;
    // Attribute off rather than width zeroed: these elements are persisted across
    // navigation, so a stale inline style would ride along for the rest of the visit.
    bar.querySelector<HTMLElement>('.territory-mine')?.removeAttribute('style');
  }

  /**
   * Say something, for `LINE_MS`, anchored above the cat.
   *
   * Not tracked per frame: the ribbon is placed once, where the cat was when it spoke.
   * A label that follows a leaping cat around is a label nobody can read, and the line
   * outliving the pounce it belongs to is the point.
   */
  function say(id: string, ms = LINE_MS): void {
    const node = el('cat-ribbon');
    if (!node) return;
    arena.lastLine = id;
    arena.spokeAt = performance.now();
    node.textContent = lineText(id);
    node.hidden = false;
    // measure after the text is in, or the first line of a fight is placed as if empty
    const cat = catEl()?.getBoundingClientRect();
    const w = node.offsetWidth;
    const x = cat ? cat.left + cat.width / 2 - w / 2 : 24;
    const y = cat ? cat.top - node.offsetHeight - 8 : 24;
    node.style.transform = `translate(${Math.max(8, Math.min(document.documentElement.clientWidth - w - 8, x)).toFixed(0)}px, ${Math.max(8, y).toFixed(0)}px)`;
    // `hidden` is re-cleared inside the frame, not only above it. The fade-out below removes
    // `show` at `ms` and hides the node 220ms later, guarded on `show` being absent — but a
    // line said inside that 220ms window sets `hidden = false` and then waits a frame to add
    // `show`, and if the hide timer lands in that gap the guard sees no `show`, hides the node,
    // and the new line is spoken invisibly. Asserting it here makes the order stop mattering.
    requestAnimationFrame(() => {
      node.hidden = false;
      node.classList.add('show');
    });
    clearTimeout(arena.lineTimer);
    arena.lineTimer = window.setTimeout(() => {
      node.classList.remove('show');
      setTimeout(() => {
        if (!node.classList.contains('show')) node.hidden = true;
      }, 220);
    }, ms);
  }

  function hushRibbon(): void {
    const node = el('cat-ribbon');
    clearTimeout(arena.lineTimer);
    if (!node) return;
    node.classList.remove('show');
    node.hidden = true;
    node.removeAttribute('style');
    node.textContent = '';
  }

  /** Gather what the cat is reacting to. */
  /**
   * A snapshot of how the fight is going, for the dialogue table and the mood.
   *
   * `consume` exists for exactly one field, and getting it wrong was a bug worth recording.
   * §7.3's last stand is an *event* rather than a state (see `FightState.lastStand`), and the first
   * version cleared the flag inside this function — "consumed by whoever reads it first". But this
   * function is called every frame by `stepMood`, and `stepTalk` returns **before** calling it
   * whenever §8's talk gap is still open. So the mood pass ate the event on the very next frame
   * and threw it away, and the climax line could only ever be said if the tier happened to be
   * entered in one of the ~16ms windows where the cat was already free to speak — otherwise the
   * loudest line in the game was silently dropped. Only the dialogue consumes it now, so the event
   * survives the gap and is said late rather than not at all; the sound sting still marks the exact
   * moment, since it fires from `stepMood` where the clock actually changes.
   */
  function fightState(
    now: number,
    ending?: FightState['ending'],
    consume = false,
  ): FightState {
    return {
      territory: territory(arena.claimed.length, arena.order.length),
      ammo: ammo().length,
      spent: arena.spent,
      freed: arena.freed.length,
      interrupts: arena.interrupts,
      idleMs: scrub.seen ? now - scrub.seen : 0,
      staleMs: now - arena.movedAt,
      collared: !!catEl()?.classList.contains('lv6'),
      mood: boss.mood,
      rung: arena.rung,
      // The found-set as of this fight's opening, which is what the snapshot is. Counting
      // `.got` here instead would return the *ammo* — already reduced by the rung and by
      // spending — and "is there another paw left to take" would go false the moment you
      // threw your last treat.
      found: arena.ammoWas.filter(Boolean).length,
      // Which game this is, for the two lines in §8 that teach a verb (§15).
      commanding: commanding(),
      /*
       * The event, handed over once (§7.3, 1.4). `stepMood` raises it on the way into the tier and
       * the dialogue takes it — see `consume` above. A boolean that stayed true for the whole tier
       * instead would be the territory-band bug again in a different type: the tier is entered at
       * ≤20% and lasts, so a *state* here shadows every rattled line for the rest of the fight.
       */
      lastStand: (() => {
        const raised = boss.lastStand;
        if (consume) boss.lastStand = false;
        return raised;
      })(),
      ending,
    };
  }

  /**
   * Re-read how the fight is going (§7.3).
   *
   * Fed its own previous answer, which is the hysteresis rather than a shortcut — see
   * `mood` in `arena.ts`. Called once a frame, before anything consumes the tier, so the
   * boss and the ribbon are never a frame apart about what kind of fight this is.
   */
  function stepMood(now: number): void {
    const next = moodFor(fightState(now), boss.mood);
    if (next === boss.mood) return;
    boss.mood = next;
    /*
     * §7.3's last stand (1.4): raise the event exactly once, on the way in. `stepMood` is the only
     * place that knows the tier *changed*, and the faster regrow clock starts on this same tick.
     *
     * Dropped again on the way *out*, because the event can now outlive the frame it was raised in
     * (see `fightState`'s `consume`) and a line about the walls closing has no business arriving
     * after they have opened again. The board can push the cat back over the line on its own — the
     * last stand regrows the very territory that ends it, which is the tier measuring itself out
     * of existence.
     */
    if (next === 'desperate') {
      boss.lastStand = true;
      playCue('laststand');
    } else {
      boss.lastStand = false;
    }
    // Published the same way and for the same reasons as `data-stance`: the tier is a real
    // piece of fight state, it is worth being able to see from outside the closure, and a
    // stylesheet may eventually want it. Written only on change — this runs every frame.
    const root = catEl();
    if (root) root.dataset.mood = next;
  }

  /** One frame of deciding whether to speak. */
  function stepTalk(now: number): void {
    /*
     * The first line of a fight comes early — OPENING_LINE_MS, inside the opening grace —
     * so a confused player hears the teach line while the cat provably cannot pounce.
     * The 1.3 playtest found the alternative: with no line due in the opening state, the
     * first thing the cat ever said was support-idle's "you can stop any time." at 8s.
     * After the first line the mood-paced gap applies as usual.
     */
    const gap = arena.lastLine
      ? LINE_MS + talkGap(aggression(boss.mood))
      : OPENING_LINE_MS;
    if (now - arena.spokeAt < gap) return;
    // The one consuming read: this is the only place a `[PH]`-free event like the last stand can
    // actually become words, so it is the only place entitled to spend it.
    const id = pickLine(fightState(now, undefined, true), arena.lastLine);
    if (id) say(id);
  }

  function stepBoss(now: number, dt: number, progress: number): void {
    const root = catEl();
    if (!root) return;

    // Stances bend durations, they do not replace them (§9.3). A bell's doubled telegraph
    // rides on top of the stance's own multiplier and is spent when the wind-up ends.
    const spec = STANCES[boss.stance];
    /*
     * §7.3's tier, times §15's round.
     *
     * The second half of "faster board and a meaner cat", and it needs no new machinery because
     * aggression already owns the four things that make a cat feel meaner (wind-up, walking speed,
     * willingness to commit, how often it talks) and §7.3 already clamps the ones that must not
     * scale. In particular `telegraphScale` only ever *shortens* the wind-up and floors it above
     * human reaction time, so a round-30 cat cannot become unreadable — §10's invariant protects
     * the escalation for free, which is why this is one multiplication rather than a system.
     *
     * `roundAggro` is 1 in round 1, so manual mode is untouched.
     */
    const aggro = aggression(boss.mood) * roundAggro(arena.round);
    const scale =
      boss.phase === 'telegraph'
        ? // §7.3's desperate tier, and it only ever shortens this — see `telegraphScale`.
          // Recovery is deliberately absent from this list: scaling it too breaks §10's
          // whiff invariant for siege, and a dodge has to stay worth making precisely
          // when the cat is desperate.
          spec.telegraph * boss.telegraphMul * telegraphScale(aggro)
        : boss.phase === 'recover'
          ? spec.recover
          : boss.phase === 'eat'
            ? boss.lureMs / LURE_MS
            : 1;
    const advance = nextPhase(boss.phase, now - boss.since, scale);
    if (advance === 'leap') {
      // The target was locked when the telegraph began (see `aim` below) — nothing
      // is decided here. That is the whole point: the wind-up is a commitment, so
      // the 420ms of it is a window you can actually dodge in.
      boss.telegraphMul = 1; // the bell's gift is spent on the wind-up it read
      if (boss.feint) {
        // A trickster's bluff: it gathers, thinks better of it, and goes back to stalking.
        // No leap, no recovery — the cost of a feint is the time, which is what makes
        // spending a treat on one feel like being had.
        boss.feint = false;
        /*
         * §9.3's double tell (1.4). Sometimes the bluff goes *straight* into the real thing.
         *
         * §9.3 says the trickster's tell "can be learned, which means it has to be missable the
         * first few times" — and once you have learned that a shallow gather means nothing, you
         * relax. This is the stance that punishes relaxing, so a third of its feints re-commit
         * immediately instead of returning to the stalk.
         *
         * The aim is re-locked here rather than reused, and that keeps §5.3's promise intact: a
         * wind-up is a commitment to a *place*, and the second one is a second commitment you
         * get the full telegraph to read. What is taken away is the safety of having read the
         * first one — not the ability to read this one.
         *
         * `!arena.ending` for §7.4's rule, and the distinction matters here: 1.1 lets a pounce
         * *already in flight* land during the ending because it was committed beforehand — but a
         * feint is the opposite of a commitment, it is the cat explicitly declining to jump. So a
         * bluff that re-commits inside the ending beat would be starting something new, which is
         * exactly what "one element, then it leaves" forbids.
         */
        if (
          Math.random() < TRICKSTER_DOUBLE &&
          !arena.ending &&
          arena.claimed.length > 0 &&
          quarry.inside
        ) {
          const moving = now - quarry.at < 90;
          const aim = predict(
            quarry.x,
            quarry.y,
            moving ? quarry.vx : 0,
            moving ? quarry.vy : 0,
            AIM_LEAD_MS,
          );
          boss.ax = boss.x;
          boss.ay = boss.y;
          boss.bx = aim.x;
          boss.by = aim.y;
          bossPhase(root, 'telegraph', now);
          return;
        }
        bossPhase(root, 'stalk', now);
        return;
      }
      bossPhase(root, 'leap', now);
    } else if (advance === 'recover') {
      bossPhase(root, 'recover', now);
      land(root, now);
    } else if (advance === 'stalk' && boss.phase === 'eat') {
      finishEating(root, now);
    } else if (advance) {
      bossPhase(root, advance, now);
    }

    if (boss.phase === 'leap') {
      const t = (now - boss.since) / LEAP_MS;
      const p = leapPos(boss.ax, boss.ay, boss.bx, boss.by, t);
      placeCat(root, p.x, p.y);
      return;
    }

    // Going to get it. Faster than stalking, and — the whole value of the resource —
    // it cannot pounce from here, so this is the window the throw bought.
    if (boss.phase === 'fetch') {
      const t = arena.treat;
      if (!t) {
        bossPhase(root, 'stalk', now);
        return;
      }
      const tx = t.x - boss.x;
      const ty = t.y - boss.y;
      const away = Math.hypot(tx, ty);
      if (away <= FETCH_REACH) {
        /*
         * §9.5, applied at the moment it starts eating. Every effect is a number on the
         * boss, never a new mechanic: how long it is occupied, how long of that it cannot
         * pounce, whether it is pinned near the spot afterwards, and two modifiers it
         * carries away with it.
         */
        const spec = treatSpec(t.kind);
        boss.lureMs = spec.lureMs;
        boss.immuneMs = spec.immuneMs;
        boss.anchorX = t.x;
        boss.anchorY = t.y;
        boss.anchorPx = spec.anchorPx;
        boss.anchorUntil = spec.anchorMs ? now + spec.lureMs + spec.anchorMs : 0;
        boss.telegraphMul = spec.telegraphMul;
        boss.slowMul = spec.slowMul;
        boss.slowUntil = spec.slowMs ? now + spec.lureMs + spec.slowMs : 0;
        throwEl()?.classList.add('eaten');
        bossPhase(root, 'eat', now);
        return;
      }
      const step = Math.min(away, FETCH_SPEED * dt);
      placeCat(root, boss.x + (tx / away) * step, boss.y + (ty / away) * step);
      faceToward(root, tx);
      return;
    }

    /*
     * Eating with the immunity already spent (§9.5's biscuit): it is still at the treat, but
     * it can pounce again. Handled by leaving the `eat` phase early while the anchor keeps
     * it in place — one mechanism, two treats, and the reason "shortest immunity but stays
     * put longest" is not the contradiction it reads as.
     */
    if (boss.phase === 'eat' && boss.immuneMs < boss.lureMs && now - boss.since >= boss.immuneMs) {
      finishEating(root, now);
    }

    // telegraph, recover and eat all hold still — a crouch, a faceplant, and a cat
    // with its head down in something better than you
    if (boss.phase !== 'stalk') return;

    // A landed treat outranks the cursor: food first, which is exactly what makes the
    // throw a decision rather than a distraction.
    if (arena.treat?.landed) {
      bossPhase(root, 'fetch', now);
      return;
    }

    /*
     * §7.3's bored tell: the cat stops chasing you and washes.
     *
     * A pause, not just an animation. "Gets bored, grooms itself" is only legible if the
     * cat visibly gives up on you for a moment — a head-wash performed while still
     * marching at your cursor reads as a glitch, not as indifference. So this holds it
     * still and takes the pounce away for the duration, which is also the mercy the tier
     * exists to deliver.
     *
     * `.grooming` and its keyframe are SiteCat's, already written for affection tier 3
     * (§0: nothing new is drawn). Re-added through a reflow because a non-looping CSS
     * animation will not replay for a class that is already present — the same trick the
     * arena note's nudge uses.
     */
    if (boss.mood === 'bored' && now >= boss.groomUntil && now - boss.groomedAt > GROOM_EVERY_MS) {
      boss.groomedAt = now;
      boss.groomUntil = now + GROOM_MS;
      root.classList.remove('grooming');
      void root.offsetWidth;
      root.classList.add('grooming');
    }
    if (now < boss.groomUntil) {
      // Busy. Not stalking, not pouncing.
      if (boss.mood !== 'bored') endGrooming(root);
      else return;
    } else {
      endGrooming(root);
    }

    // Nothing to stalk. In manual mode that is a pointer outside the window (§5.3); in commander
    // mode it means the squad has not been dealt yet, which is one frame at the start of a round.
    if (!quarry.inside || quarry.x < 0) return;
    const dx = quarry.x - boss.x;
    const dy = quarry.y - boss.y;
    const dist = Math.hypot(dx, dy);

    if (
      canPounce(boss.phase) &&
      /*
       * Nothing new is started once the fight is decided (§7.4).
       *
       * The ending is a *beat*, not a stop: the loop keeps running for `WIN_BEAT_MS` so the cat
       * can take one thing back and walk off, and everything except the dialogue kept running
       * with it. So the cat could wind up and pounce again inside its own exit, taking a second
       * and third element after the one the ending is built around — and on a loss it kept
       * hunting somebody who had already lost. §7.4 asks for "one element, then it leaves", and
       * one is the whole gesture. A pounce already in flight is allowed to land: it was
       * committed before the fight was decided, and freezing a cat mid-air to enforce a rule
       * about starting things would be a worse lie than the one being fixed.
       */
      !arena.ending &&
      now - arena.openedAt > OPENING_GRACE_MS &&
      // The stance's patience and the mood's, composed and clamped (§7.3): a bored cat
      // waits until you have nearly finished before it can be bothered, a desperate one
      // commits as soon as you have started.
      provoked(dist, progress, patienceFor(spec.patience, aggro)) &&
      arena.claimed.length > 0
    ) {
      /*
       * Commit. Everything about the pounce is decided here and nothing after.
       *
       * Velocity is only meaningful while the quarry is moving: a cursor that
       * stopped keeps its last sampled reading, because nothing fires to say "still
       * now", and aiming ahead of it would make the cat miss the one player it is
       * certainly entitled to hit — the one holding perfectly still. A kitten stamps
       * `at` the same way while it walks and zeroes its velocity on arrival, so the
       * rule reads identically against either quarry (§15).
       */
      const moving = now - quarry.at < 90;
      const aim = predict(
        quarry.x,
        quarry.y,
        moving ? quarry.vx : 0,
        moving ? quarry.vy : 0,
        AIM_LEAD_MS,
      );
      boss.ax = boss.x;
      boss.ay = boss.y;
      boss.bx = aim.x;
      boss.by = aim.y;
      boss.feint = Math.random() < spec.feint;
      bossPhase(root, 'telegraph', now);
      return;
    }

    if (dist > 1) {
      const slowed = now < boss.slowUntil ? boss.slowMul : 1;
      // Mood is the fourth multiplier here, after the stance, the yarn and the frame: a
      // bored cat ambles, a desperate one hurries. Unlike the telegraph this one scales
      // both ways — walking slowly costs the player nothing, so there is no invariant to
      // protect, and an ambling cat is the most legible tell the bored tier has after
      // the grooming.
      const step = Math.min(dist, STALK_SPEED * spec.stalk * slowed * aggro * dt);
      let nx = boss.x + (dx / dist) * step;
      let ny = boss.y + (dy / dist) * step;
      // Anchored by a feather (or the tail of a biscuit): it will come this far and no
      // further, which is what makes a thrown treat a *place* rather than a delay.
      if (now < boss.anchorUntil && boss.anchorPx > 0) {
        const ax = nx - boss.anchorX;
        const ay = ny - boss.anchorY;
        const away = Math.hypot(ax, ay);
        if (away > boss.anchorPx) {
          nx = boss.anchorX + (ax / away) * boss.anchorPx;
          ny = boss.anchorY + (ay / away) * boss.anchorPx;
        }
      }
      // Siege never leaves the floor: it moves in x only, so it is barely a threat and the
      // board has to do its fighting for it.
      if (spec.pin) ny = boss.y;
      placeCat(root, nx, ny);
      faceToward(root, dx);
    }

    // Siege's other half: the page grows back — but not once the fight is decided, for the same
    // reason the pounce above stops committing. A siege win beat otherwise regrew the board
    // under the very line saying the cat was taking one thing and leaving.
    if (
      spec.regrowMs > 0 &&
      !arena.ending &&
      now > boss.regrowAt &&
      arena.claimed.length < arena.order.length
    ) {
      /*
       * §7.3's last stand (1.4): the clock, not just the wind-up.
       *
       * 0.9 gave the desperate tier a faster telegraph and 1.0 then measured that the fight's
       * real counter is *fleeing* — so the tier was escalating the one threat a good player has
       * already opted out of, while the regrow clock that does reach a distant player stayed
       * fixed. The fight was calmest exactly where it should tighten. `regrowInterval` is where
       * that is fixed, and it lives in `arena.ts` so the bound against §9.4's floor is testable.
       *
       * The interval is charged at the *end* of this block rather than here, because what it costs
       * now depends on what the tick took — see the sweep below and `regrowInterval`'s third
       * argument. Charged either way, taken or not: a tick that finds nothing to take still has to
       * push the clock forward, or the block re-runs every frame for the rest of the fight.
       */
      let took = 0;
      const back = takeGround();
      if (back) {
        took = 1;
        claim(back, arena.order.indexOf(back), arena.seed);
        arena.claimed.push(back);
        arena.total = Math.max(arena.total, arena.claimed.length);
        /*
         * §9.3's siege sweep (1.4): every other tick, the walls come in from two sides.
         *
         * Siege's premise is "it cannot leave the floor, so the board does its fighting for it",
         * and that was one clock and nothing else — the most distinctive stance had the least
         * distinctive moment. `sweepPartner` picks the neighbour *in document order*, so the pair
         * is adjacent page furniture and reads as a wall moving rather than as the clock ticking
         * twice. On a beat rather than every tick, so it can be anticipated.
         */
        boss.regrows++;
        if (spec.pin && boss.regrows % SIEGE_SWEEP_EVERY === 0) {
          const held = arena.claimed.map((n) => arena.order.indexOf(n)).filter((i) => i >= 0);
          const partner = sweepPartner(arena.order.indexOf(back), held, arena.order.length);
          const alsoTake = partner >= 0 ? arena.order[partner] : undefined;
          if (alsoTake?.isConnected && !arena.claimed.includes(alsoTake)) {
            /*
             * **Out of `freed` before into `claimed`.** The two lists are disjoint by contract and
             * `takeGround` relies on it: it pops `freed` and returns without checking `claimed`,
             * so an element left in both gets claimed a *second* time later — and `open()` already
             * writes down what that costs, because `claim()` snapshots the element's style into
             * `wasStyled` and claiming an already-claimed node therefore records the **tilted**
             * style as the original, which `free()` then faithfully restores. That is pillar 2
             * failing, from a bookkeeping slip two lines long.
             *
             * The first version of this sweep took its partner straight out of `arena.order` and
             * skipped this, which is what turned a green `arena8` into 11/13.
             */
            const wasFreed = arena.freed.indexOf(alsoTake);
            if (wasFreed >= 0) arena.freed.splice(wasFreed, 1);
            claim(alsoTake, partner, arena.seed);
            arena.claimed.push(alsoTake);
            arena.total = Math.max(arena.total, arena.claimed.length);
            took = 2;
          }
        }
        arena.movedAt = now;
        caption(arenaCaption(arena.claimed.length, arena.total));
        paintTerritory();
        // §15 first: in commander mode a full board is a round to start again, not a defeat, and
        // the two rules disagree on purpose (see `roundOutcome`). Manual mode keeps §2's loss,
        // ammo and all.
        if (!checkRound() && isLost(arena.claimed.length, arena.order.length, ammo().length))
          finish('lose', now);
      }
      /*
       * The sweep's bill. Two claims cost two intervals, so §9.3's moment is paid for in *time*
       * and the board's rate is exactly what 1.3 measured as winnable. Found the hard way: the
       * version that took two for free plateaued a fleeing player at six claims for ten
       * exchanges — see `regrowInterval`.
       */
      // §15: the same bill, with the round's own multiplier and floor folded in. Manual mode passes
      // round 1, where `roundRegrowMs` is `regrowInterval` exactly — so 1.4's measurements still
      // describe the fight a manual player gets.
      boss.regrowAt = now + roundRegrowMs(spec.regrowMs, boss.mood, took, arena.round);
    }
  }

  /**
   * Touchdown. Hit or miss is decided against where the cat *aimed*.
   *
   * A hit costs you the hold and one element back; §7.2 still applies, so it costs
   * nothing outside this fight. A miss costs the cat `RECOVER_MS` plus the walk
   * back from wherever you were standing when it committed — which is the real
   * reward for dodging, and why the player choosing *where* to flee matters.
   */
  function land(root: HTMLElement, now: number): void {
    placeCat(root, boss.bx, boss.by);
    // 1.3: the landing has a visual (the faceplant), so it has a sound. Hit or miss
    // both land — the thud is the pounce arriving, not the catch.
    playCue('land');
    const caught = quarry.inside && pounceHit(boss.bx, boss.by, quarry.x, quarry.y);
    if (!caught) return;

    // the hold is gone, not reduced: partial retention would remove the reason to
    // ever dodge (§5.2)
    dropHold(quarry);
    quarry.since = now;
    hideRing();
    arena.interrupts++;
    /*
     * §15: the kitten flinches, and that is the whole visible cost — the hold above is the real one.
     *
     * `seek` is deliberately *kept*. Clearing it sends the kitten shopping for a new claim after
     * every hit, which on a board with one claim left means walking away from the only thing worth
     * holding; keeping it means the kitten picks itself up and tries the same thing again, which is
     * exactly what a player does and what 0.7 measured them winning with.
     */
    if (commanding()) {
      const hit = squad.find((k) => k === quarry);
      if (hit) hit.flinchUntil = now + KITTEN_FLINCH_MS;
    }

    /*
     * **A landed pounce takes ground from a player and only tempo from a kitten (§15).**
     *
     * `RECLAIM_ON_HIT` exists because a human who never gets hit would face no pressure at all
     * (0.6's note: a loss has to be reachable). A kitten is the opposite case — it *cannot* dodge,
     * because holding still is the verb, so every pounce that lands is a free claim for the cat.
     *
     * Measured, that is not a difficulty knob, it is a hole in the arithmetic. `squad.ts` proves the
     * squad out-reclaims the board by comparing two rates, and the board's rate in that proof is the
     * regrow clock — the thing `MIN_REGROW_MS` floors and the escalation tunes. Ground taken by
     * pounces is a *second*, unpriced source of board growth, and with it the browser showed exactly
     * what the proof could not see: one kitten, round 1, seventy-two seconds, three claims to five
     * and back, the round never turning over.
     *
     * So in commander mode a pounce keeps the one thing it costs the squad — **the hold**, which can
     * be most of `KITTEN_WORK_MS` of work thrown away — and the *board* does the board's job, which is
     * what §9.3 says it is for. Manual mode is untouched: a player can dodge, so a player pays in
     * ground.
     */
    for (let i = 0; i < (commanding() ? 0 : RECLAIM_ON_HIT); i++) {
      const back = takeGround();
      if (!back) break;
      claim(back, arena.order.indexOf(back), arena.seed);
      arena.claimed.push(back);
      arena.total = Math.max(arena.total, arena.claimed.length);
    }
    /*
     * §9.3's ambush pin (1.4): a hit is a *position*, not just a subtraction.
     *
     * Ambush is "the one that actually hunts you", and until now a landed pounce paid it exactly
     * what siege gets for a clock tick — one element — despite costing it a read, a commitment
     * and a whiff risk. So it now stands over what it took: re-scrubbing that claim means
     * waiting the pin out or spending a treat to lure it off, which turns a hit into the kind of
     * problem the player has verbs for.
     *
     * Reuses `boss.anchor*` exactly as the biscuit and feather do (see `stepTreat`), so this is a
     * new *trigger* on existing machinery rather than a new system — and it therefore inherits
     * the leash logic in `stepBoss` for free. Ambush only: siege is already pinned by `spec.pin`,
     * sleepy is a gift, and giving the trickster a second signature would blur the one it has.
     */
    if (boss.stance === 'ambush') {
      boss.anchorX = boss.bx;
      boss.anchorY = boss.by;
      boss.anchorPx = AMBUSH_PIN_PX;
      boss.anchorUntil = now + AMBUSH_PIN_MS;
    }
    arena.movedAt = now;
    caption(arenaCaption(arena.claimed.length, arena.total));
    paintTerritory();
    // §15: a landed pounce can fill the board too, and in commander mode that starts the round
    // again rather than ending the fight.
    if (!checkRound() && isLost(arena.claimed.length, arena.order.length, ammo().length))
      finish('lose', now);
  }

  /**
   * What a landed pounce takes.
   *
   * The most recently freed element first — taking back the thing you just earned is
   * legible as cause and effect in a way a random element is not. But **a loss has to be
   * reachable**, and until 0.6 this only ever took from the freed pile: a player who had
   * freed nothing could not fall below the opening 55%, so territory could never reach
   * 100% and §2's losing condition could not fire. Failing that, the cat takes the thing
   * it just landed on, which is the most legible claim available; failing *that*, the
   * nearest unclaimed thing.
   */
  function takeGround(): HTMLElement | null {
    while (arena.freed.length) {
      const back = arena.freed.pop();
      // `!arena.claimed.includes` added in 1.4, as a belt to the braces one line up in the siege
      // sweep. The two lists are disjoint by contract, and for eight versions nothing could break
      // that because `stepScrub` was the only writer. 1.4 added a second writer and immediately
      // broke it — so the reader now refuses a double-claim rather than trusting the invariant,
      // because the cost of trusting it is `claim()` snapshotting a tilted style as the original.
      if (back?.isConnected && !arena.claimed.includes(back)) return back;
    }
    const free = arena.order.filter((n) => n.isConnected && !arena.claimed.includes(n));
    if (!free.length) return null;
    const under = document.elementFromPoint(boss.bx, boss.by);
    const landedOn = under instanceof Element ? free.find((n) => n === under || n.contains(under)) : null;
    if (landedOn) return landedOn;
    let best = free[0]!;
    let bestD = Infinity;
    for (const n of free) {
      const r = n.getBoundingClientRect();
      const d = Math.hypot(r.left + r.width / 2 - boss.bx, r.top + r.height / 2 - boss.by);
      if (d < bestD) {
        bestD = d;
        best = n;
      }
    }
    return best;
  }

  /* ---------------------------------------------------------------- *
   * Endings (§7, §8.4)
   * ---------------------------------------------------------------- */

  /**
   * End the fight with a beat rather than a cut.
   *
   * The win beat is §7.4's hook: the cat takes **one** element back and leaves, so the
   * fight ends on unfinished business instead of a fanfare. It is theatre and not a
   * state — the page is restored whole a second later, because a byte-identical restore
   * is pillar 2 and is not negotiable for the sake of a joke.
   */
  function finish(ending: 'win' | 'lose', now: number): void {
    if (arena.ending) return;
    arena.ending = ending;
    // 1.3: each ending has a visual (the parting line, the restored page) and a sound.
    playCue(ending === 'win' ? 'win' : 'lose');
    /*
     * The line is chosen *before* the rung moves, and the order is the whole of §9.4's voice.
     *
     * `fightState` reports the rung this fight was fought on, so "again? i keep one back."
     * fires on a win while `rung < found` — the cat naming terms it has not yet imposed. Move
     * the ladder first and the line would describe the next fight instead of offering it, and
     * a win at the top rung would announce a paw it had already taken.
     */
    const line = pickLine(fightState(now, ending), undefined);
    if (line) say(line, ending === 'win' ? WIN_BEAT_MS : LOSE_BEAT_MS);

    // §9.4: up on a win, down on a loss. A truce never comes through here, which is why
    // `nextRung` leaves the rung alone for one — an interruption is not a result.
    arena.rung = nextRung(arena.rung, ending, arena.ammoWas.filter(Boolean).length);

    if (ending === 'win') {
      // the notch, for the rest of the session (§7.1)
      catEl()?.classList.add('notched');
      const parting = takeGround();
      if (parting) {
        claim(parting, arena.order.indexOf(parting), arena.seed);
        arena.claimed.push(parting);
        paintTerritory();
      }
    }
    /*
     * Held so it can be cancelled, which it was not until 1.1.
     *
     * The beat closes the fight a couple of seconds after an ending. If the visitor closes it
     * themselves inside that window and then opens a *new* fight, the old timer was still
     * armed — and fired into the new fight, shutting it a moment after it started. Reachable
     * on the instant-swap path (§14.6), where a close and a re-open both cost nothing.
     *
     * A timer that outlives the thing it was scheduled for is the same class of bug as a class
     * that outlives the fight, and `lineTimer` was already being cleared for exactly this
     * reason two lines away.
     */
    clearTimeout(arena.beatTimer);
    arena.beatTimer = window.setTimeout(
      () => setArena(false),
      ending === 'win' ? WIN_BEAT_MS : LOSE_BEAT_MS,
    );
  }

  /* ---------------------------------------------------------------- *
   * On / off — one code path, so the DOM can only be restored one way
   * ---------------------------------------------------------------- */

  /**
   * The only way in or out. Esc, the toggle, a cleared board and both auto-truces
   * all come through here, so there is exactly one implementation of "restore the
   * page" — a second one is a second thing that can leave a claim behind.
   *
   * Idempotent by the first line. §13.6's latch (a press arriving *during* the
   * transition) belongs with the transition, which is step 6; an instant swap has
   * no window to land in.
   */
  /**
   * The way in and out, behind the curtain (§14).
   *
   * The transition is decoration and the state change is not, so every path here ends with
   * `open()` or `close()` having run exactly once: no canvas, no 2D context, a device too
   * slow to finish inside `WALL_MS`, a tab hidden halfway, or the visitor pressing Esc
   * during the flood. That is why step 0 built the instant swap first — this is a layer on
   * top of a thing that already worked (§14.6).
   *
   * `arena.curtain` holds the reverse handle: an interruption that has to wait for an
   * animation to finish is not an interruption.
   */
  function setArena(on: boolean): void {
    if (on === arena.want) return;
    arena.want = on;
    // The button answers the press, not the animation.
    syncToggle();
    arena.curtain?.();
    arena.curtain = null;
    const canvas = el('cat-curtain') as HTMLCanvasElement | null;
    const swap = (behindCurtain: boolean) => {
      if (on) open(behindCurtain);
      else close();
      syncToggle();
    };
    if (!canvas || motionReduced()) {
      // §14.5: never a half-speed flood. Under reduced motion the arena does not open at
      // all today (§11), so this is really the no-canvas path.
      swap(false);
      return;
    }
    arena.curtain = runCurtain(canvas, on ? 'in' : 'out', () => swap(true));
  }

  /**
   * A truce the visitor asked for, which gets its own line (§8.4).
   *
   * Said *before* closing, because `say` anchors the ribbon to where the cat is and the
   * cat goes back to walking the moment the fight ends. The line then outlives the fight
   * by design: the page is already restored while the cat is still muttering about it,
   * which is the only place in the build where the ribbon is deliberately louder than
   * the game.
   */
  /**
   * End it with nothing said.
   *
   * The auto-truces, reduce-motion, and navigating away are not the visitor choosing to
   * stop, so the cat gets no parting shot — a line like "sensible." on a page you
   * *didn't* leave deliberately would be the cat taking credit for a timer. This is also
   * the only path that hushes the ribbon, since the deliberate ones are said on the way
   * out and have to outlive the fight.
   */
  function endQuietly(): void {
    hushRibbon();
    setArena(false);
  }

  function truce(): void {
    if (!arena.want) return;
    if (!arena.on) {
      // Esc during the flood. There is no fight yet to have an opinion about how it went,
      // so this is a cancelled commitment rather than a truce, and the cat says nothing.
      setArena(false);
      return;
    }
    if (arena.ending) {
      // an ending is already playing; let it have the last word
      setArena(false);
      return;
    }
    const kind = boss.phase === 'recover' ? 'truce-recover' : 'truce';
    const line = pickLine(fightState(performance.now(), kind), undefined);
    if (line) say(line, LINE_MS);
    setArena(false);
  }

  /**
   * `behindCurtain` is not cosmetic bookkeeping: it is the difference between the bar
   * sweeping into a reveal the visitor can see and sweeping behind an opaque sheet.
   */
  function open(behindCurtain = false): void {
    /*
     * A second open over a live fight would replace `arena.claimed` wholesale and leave the
     * first board claimed with nothing tracking it — and worse, `claim()` snapshots the
     * element's style into `wasStyled`, so claiming an already-claimed node records the
     * *tilted* style as the original and `free()` faithfully restores the tilt. Closing first
     * costs nothing on the normal path and makes that unreachable rather than unlikely.
     */
    if (arena.on) close();
    const now = performance.now();
    arena.round = 1;
    arena.best = readBestRound();
    if (!dealBoard(now, behindCurtain)) {
      // Nothing to fight over. The intent has to be given back too, or the button stays
      // pressed over an arena that never opened — §13.5's exact failure state.
      arena.want = false;
      return;
    }
    arena.on = true;
    document.documentElement.classList.add('cat-arena-on');
    arena.openedAt = now;
    arena.spokeAt = now;
    arena.spent = 0;
    arena.lastLine = '';
    scrub.seen = now;
    dropHold(scrub);
    const paws = [...document.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>('#cat-score .cat-paw[data-slug]')];
    arena.ammoWas = paws.map((p) => p.classList.contains('got'));
    withholdForRung(paws);
    takeCat(now);
    if (commanding()) dealSquad(now);
    arena.raf = requestAnimationFrame(tick);
  }

  /**
   * Deal a board on the page as it is now — the opening of a fight, and of every round after it
   * (§15).
   *
   * Extracted from `open()` in 2.0 so a cleared round can start the next one without going through
   * the curtain: commander mode's rounds are *inside* one fight, and re-opening the arena would
   * flood the screen with ink every twenty seconds and reset the round counter it exists to grow.
   *
   * Re-queries `candidates()` each time rather than reusing the first board, because the visitor
   * may have scrolled or navigated between rounds and §9.1's promise is that the arena is whatever
   * page you are on. Returns false when the page has nothing claimable, which the callers treat as
   * "do not open" and "stop here" respectively.
   */
  function dealBoard(now: number, behindCurtain = false): boolean {
    const board = candidates();
    if (!board.length) return false;
    const seed = (Date.now() ^ (Math.random() * 0xffffffff)) >>> 0;
    const picked = pickClaims(board.length, INITIAL_CLAIM_FRACTION, seed);
    arena.order = board;
    arena.seed = seed;
    arena.claimed = picked.map((i) => board[i]!);
    arena.freed = [];
    arena.total = arena.claimed.length;
    // index by position in the *board*, not in the claimed subset, so a re-claim
    // after a landed pounce reproduces the tilt the element had the first time
    arena.claimed.forEach((node) => claim(node, board.indexOf(node), seed));
    caption(arenaCaption(arena.claimed.length, arena.total));
    arena.movedAt = now;
    arena.interrupts = 0;
    arena.ending = null;
    arena.roundAt = now;
    /*
     * Published for the same reason `data-mood` and `data-stance` are (§7.3, 1.3): a round is real
     * fight state, and a harness that cannot read it has to *infer* which of two outcomes happened
     * from a caption — which is exactly the "assert on a proxy" trap §12 keeps recording. It also
     * tells the difference between the two things that both deal a fresh board: a round that was
     * cleared and a round that was lost, which look identical from outside.
     */
    // Commander mode only: a manual fight has rungs rather than rounds (§9.4), and publishing a
    // round number over it would describe a clock that is not running.
    if (commanding()) document.documentElement.dataset.catRound = String(arena.round);
    else delete document.documentElement.dataset.catRound;
    paintTerritory(behindCurtain);
    return true;
  }

  /* ---------------------------------------------------------------- *
   * Rounds (§15) — endless, and there is no losing
   * ---------------------------------------------------------------- */

  /**
   * The board came back. Bank the round and deal a harder one.
   *
   * §7.4's win beat is kept and shortened into a *breath* rather than an ending: the cat does not
   * get the parting shot here, because it has not gone anywhere — the next round is the same fight
   * continuing. What the visitor gets instead is the number going up, which is the whole reward
   * commander mode has, and the reason it is the thing that persists.
   */
  function clearRound(now: number): void {
    const cleared = arena.round;
    const seconds = ((now - arena.roundAt) / 1000).toFixed(0);
    arena.round = cleared + 1;
    // §15's one stored fact. `writeBestRound` is monotonic, so this can only ever be good news.
    const record = writeBestRound(arena.round);
    arena.best = Math.max(arena.best, arena.round);
    playCue('win');
    caption(
      record
        ? `round ${cleared} cleared in ${seconds}s — best yet`
        : `round ${cleared} cleared in ${seconds}s`,
    );
    say(record ? 'round-record' : 'round-clear', ROUND_BEAT_MS);
    /*
     * A beat, then the next board — and `arena.ending` is deliberately *not* set.
     *
     * `ending` means "this fight is over and nothing new starts" (§7.4, tightened in 1.1), which is
     * exactly wrong here: the fight is not over, one round of it is. Setting it would freeze the
     * pounce and the regrow for the beat, which reads as the cat losing interest at the moment the
     * round it just lost should be turning nasty.
     */
    clearTimeout(arena.beatTimer);
    arena.beatTimer = window.setTimeout(() => {
      if (!arena.on || !commanding()) return;
      const then = performance.now();
      if (!dealBoard(then, false)) {
        // The page stopped having anything to fight over mid-run — a navigation, or content
        // swapped underneath. Nothing to escalate into, so bow out the quiet way (§11).
        endQuietly();
        return;
      }
      rollStance(arena.seed);
      dealSquad(then);
    }, ROUND_BEAT_MS);
  }

  /**
   * The cat took the whole board. Start the round again — **there is no losing** (§15).
   *
   * The round number does not move, so nothing is taken away and the record is untouched: this is
   * the T-Rex bargain, where failure costs you the run and never the score. §2's loss and §9.4's
   * ladder still exist in manual mode, where a treat in hand is a way out and the rung is the
   * stake; a commander has neither, so the honest answer to a full board is another go at it.
   */
  function restartRound(): void {
    playCue('lose');
    caption('the page is theirs — going again');
    say('round-again', ROUND_BEAT_MS);
    clearTimeout(arena.beatTimer);
    arena.beatTimer = window.setTimeout(() => {
      if (!arena.on || !commanding()) return;
      const then = performance.now();
      if (!dealBoard(then, false)) {
        endQuietly();
        return;
      }
      rollStance(arena.seed);
      dealSquad(then);
    }, ROUND_BEAT_MS);
  }

  /**
   * Ask what this board means, and act on it — commander mode's only two outcomes (§15).
   *
   * Called from the two places that can fill a board: a landed pounce and a regrow. The pure rule
   * lives in `squad.ts` and deliberately does not take ammo, which is the difference between
   * `isLost` and this: a full board is not a defeat here, it is a round to start again.
   */
  function checkRound(): boolean {
    if (!commanding() || arena.ending) return false;
    const outcome = roundOutcome(arena.claimed.length, arena.order.length);
    if (outcome === 'restart') {
      restartRound();
      return true;
    }
    return false;
  }

  /**
   * Borrow the cat from `SiteCat.astro` and start it wherever it already is.
   *
   * Starting from its real position rather than a corner matters: the fight opens
   * with the cat where the visitor last saw it, so the boss is recognisably the
   * same animal that was wandering the footer a second ago.
   */
  function takeCat(now: number): void {
    document.dispatchEvent(new CustomEvent('cat:standdown'));
    const root = catEl();
    if (!root) return;
    root.classList.add('boss');
    // SiteCat's `stop()` parked it as `idle`; a boss is not idle, and leaving the
    // class on lets its resting animations fight the phase ones. `grooming` goes for the
    // same reason and one more: SiteCat may have left one running from its own idle
    // repertoire, and a fight that opens mid-wash would inherit it.
    root.classList.remove('idle');
    endGrooming(root);
    const r = root.getBoundingClientRect();
    boss.last = now;
    boss.facing = 1;
    rollStance(arena.seed);
    placeCat(root, r.left + r.width / 2, r.top + r.height / 2);
  }

  /**
   * Roll this fight's opponent and wipe everything the last one was doing.
   *
   * Extracted from `takeCat` in 2.0, because §15's rounds need exactly this and nothing else that
   * `takeCat` does: a new round is a new *opponent* on the same page, not a re-borrowed cat. Every
   * reset in here was already load-bearing for a rematch (1.1's stale-mood note, 1.4's sweep beat),
   * and a round is a rematch that skipped the paperwork — so a round that inherited any of it would
   * reproduce bugs those notes were written about.
   */
  function rollStance(seed: number): void {
    const root = catEl();
    const now = performance.now();
    boss.stance = pickStance(seed);
    boss.feint = false;
    boss.telegraphMul = 1;
    boss.slowUntil = 0;
    boss.slowMul = 1;
    boss.anchorUntil = 0;
    boss.anchorPx = 0;
    boss.lureMs = LURE_MS;
    boss.immuneMs = LURE_MS;
    // The round's own clock, so an escalated round is already fast on its first tick rather than
    // taking one free interval at the old pace (§15).
    boss.regrowAt = now + roundRegrowMs(STANCES[boss.stance].regrowMs, 'even', 1, arena.round);
    // Reset with everything else, or a rematch inherits the last fight's sweep beat and siege's
    // first tick lands as a double for no reason the player could have read.
    boss.regrows = 0;
    boss.lastStand = false;
    // Every fight starts even, whatever the last one ended as: `mood` is hysteretic, so a
    // stale tier would carry the previous board's verdict into the opening of this one.
    boss.mood = 'even';
    boss.groomedAt = now;
    if (!root) return;
    root.dataset.mood = 'even';
    root.dataset.stance = boss.stance;
    bossPhase(root, 'stalk', now);
  }

  /** Give it back, from where the fight left it, and let it walk on. */
  function releaseCat(): void {
    const root = catEl();
    if (root) {
      root.classList.remove(
        'boss',
        'boss-telegraph',
        'boss-leap',
        'boss-recover',
        'boss-eat',
        'boss-fetch',
        'boss-feint',
        'boss-swatted',
      );
      clearTimeout(boss.swatTimer);
      boss.swatTimer = 0;
      endGrooming(root);
      delete root.dataset.stance;
      delete root.dataset.mood;
      // The round goes with them: §7.2 says a fight leaves no trace, and a `data-` attribute left on
      // <html> after the page is handed back is a trace. The stored best round is the one exception
      // (§13.4 as amended), and it lives in storage rather than on the document.
      delete document.documentElement.dataset.catRound;
      root.style.removeProperty('--boss-scale');
      document.dispatchEvent(
        new CustomEvent('cat:resume', { detail: { x: boss.x - root.offsetWidth / 2 } }),
      );
    } else {
      document.dispatchEvent(new CustomEvent('cat:resume'));
    }
  }

  /**
   * End it, with the DOM exactly as found. Pillar 2 — and the reason Esc, the
   * toggle, a cleared board and the auto-truce all come through here.
   */
  function close(): void {
    cancelAnimationFrame(arena.raf);
    arena.raf = 0;
    /*
     * The squad goes before the board does, and the order matters (§15).
     *
     * `disbandSquad` takes each kitten's progress mark off the claim it was holding. `free()` below
     * restores the whole `style` attribute from its snapshot and would wipe a stray `--scrub`
     * anyway — but only for elements that are *currently claimed*, and a kitten can be halfway
     * through something that a pounce took back a frame ago. Clearing marks by owner rather than by
     * board is the version that cannot leave one behind, which is pillar 2 and is not negotiable.
     */
    disbandSquad();
    clearAllWash();
    for (const node of arena.claimed) free(node, false);
    arena.claimed = [];
    arena.freed = [];
    arena.order = [];
    arena.total = 0;
    arena.on = false;
    clearTimeout(arena.beatTimer);
    arena.beatTimer = 0;
    /*
     * `want` is deliberately *not* touched here. It is intent, owned by `setArena` and by
     * `open`'s no-board bail; this function is mechanism. Syncing it looked like harmless
     * belt-and-braces and was the opposite: pressing the toggle during the exit transition
     * runs `setArena(true)`, which aborts the out-curtain and delivers its `close()` — so
     * `close()` would land *after* the new intent and reset it to false. The button then read
     * "off" over a fight that was opening, and the next press opened a second board on top of
     * the first, stranding the first one's `--claim-tilt` on the page.
     */
    document.documentElement.classList.remove('cat-arena-on');
    hideRing();
    dropHold(scrub);
    scrub.x = scrub.y = -1;
    scrub.vx = scrub.vy = 0;
    clearTreat();
    hideTerritory();
    releaseCat();
    arena.caption = '';
    arena.order = [];
    restoreAmmo();
    restoreCaption();
  }

  /* ---------------------------------------------------------------- *
   * The toggle (§13)
   * ---------------------------------------------------------------- */

  const toggle = () => el('cat-arena-toggle') as HTMLButtonElement | null;

  /**
   * Why the arena can't open right now, or null if it can.
   *
   * Both answers are "not yet", not "not for you", and both are stated in the
   * widget rather than by an absent button — §11's reversal, applied to the
   * second case as well as the first.
   */
  function blockedBecause(): string | null {
    if (motionReduced()) return 'Not while motion is reduced — there is no still version of this yet.';
    /*
     * The coarse-pointer refusal is **gone as of 1.2**, and what replaced it was not an input
     * adapter. §5.2 rejected touch on two grounds — a press-and-hold has no aim, and a finger
     * covers what it holds — and 1.0's flee-and-hold measurement implies a third and worse one:
     * a mouse pays travel time to reach safety and a finger teleports, so every hold would be
     * free and the fight would have no decisions at all.
     *
     * All three are answered by one fact about a phone, measured rather than assumed: the cat is
     * `position: fixed` at the bottom of the viewport and claims are in document flow, so
     * **scroll position is distance**. At 390×844 a claim held high on the screen sits 683–687px
     * from the cat (safe past `SAFE_FLEE_PX` ≈ 423px, 15 of 15 measured), centred it is 413–420px
     * (0 of 16 safe), and low it is 161–178px. Aim is back, it costs a flick, and §5.2 already
     * ruled that a scroll mid-hold interrupts it. See the 1.2 note in the GDD.
     */
    return null;
  }

  /**
   * Make the button agree with reality — every time either changes.
   *
   * The failure state §13.5 names is a button whose rendering and the arena's
   * actual state disagree, which is why this is called from `setArena` rather
   * than from the click handler: an auto-truce or a cleared board flips the
   * button back on its own, visibly, instead of leaving it pressed over a dead
   * arena.
   */
  function syncToggle(): void {
    const btn = toggle();
    if (!btn) return;
    btn.setAttribute('aria-pressed', arena.want ? 'true' : 'false');
    const why = blockedBecause();
    btn.setAttribute('aria-disabled', why ? 'true' : 'false');
    const note = el('cat-arena-note');
    if (note) {
      if (why) note.textContent = why;
      note.hidden = !why;
    }
    // Describe the button only while there is a reason to; a description that is
    // always attached but usually irrelevant is noise on every focus.
    if (why) btn.setAttribute('aria-describedby', 'cat-arena-note');
    else btn.removeAttribute('aria-describedby');
  }

  function init(): void {
    const btn = toggle();
    if (!btn || btn.dataset.init) return;
    btn.dataset.init = '1';

    // Revealing the toggle here is what stops it being a dead control: if this
    // component is ever dropped from the layout, the button never appears.
    btn.closest<HTMLElement>('.cat-arena-chip')?.removeAttribute('hidden');

    // 1.3: the sound toggle, beside the arena toggle in the same chip. Session-only,
    // off by default — setSfxEnabled creates the AudioContext inside this click (a user
    // gesture), which is the browser's autoplay gate and the consent moment at once.
    const soundBtn = el('cat-sound-toggle') as HTMLButtonElement | null;
    const syncSound = () => {
      soundBtn?.setAttribute('aria-pressed', sfxEnabled() ? 'true' : 'false');
    };
    soundBtn?.addEventListener('click', () => {
      setSfxEnabled(!sfxEnabled());
      syncSound();
    });
    syncSound();

    /*
     * 2.0: which game (§15). Pressed means manual — 1.4's fight, held by hand.
     *
     * **It only ever takes effect on the next fight**, and that is a decision rather than a
     * limitation. Swapping modes mid-fight would have to answer questions with no good answers:
     * what happens to four kittens halfway through four holds, who is the boss hunting during the
     * frame the pointer becomes a target again, and does a round counter survive becoming a fight
     * that has rungs instead. §13.5's lesson was that intent and state disagreeing is where the
     * bugs live, so the switch changes intent and the next `open()` reads it — one code path, no
     * mid-air conversions.
     */
    const modeBtn = el('cat-manual-toggle') as HTMLButtonElement | null;
    const syncMode = () => {
      modeBtn?.setAttribute('aria-pressed', mode === 'manual' ? 'true' : 'false');
    };
    modeBtn?.addEventListener('click', () => {
      mode = mode === 'manual' ? 'commander' : 'manual';
      syncMode();
      // Say what it will do, since it cannot do it yet. The HUD line is the arena's own voice
      // (§6) and this is the one place it has to describe the *future*.
      if (arena.on) caption(mode === 'manual' ? 'next fight: you play' : 'next fight: kittens play');
    });
    syncMode();

    btn.addEventListener('click', () => {
      if (blockedBecause()) {
        // `aria-disabled`, not `disabled`, so the press is ours to answer: the
        // note is already showing, so flash it rather than doing nothing at all.
        const note = el('cat-arena-note');
        if (!note) return;
        note.classList.remove('cat-arena-note-nudge');
        void note.offsetWidth; // reflow, or re-adding the class re-runs nothing
        note.classList.add('cat-arena-note-nudge');
        return;
      }
      // The toggle is the same gesture as Esc, so it gets the same parting line.
      if (arena.want) truce();
      else setArena(true);
    });

    window.addEventListener(
      'pointermove',
      (e) => {
        if (!arena.on) return;
        const now = performance.now();
        const dt = (now - scrub.at) / 1000;
        /*
         * Velocity from one pair of samples, and only over an interval short enough to
         * mean anything. A gap measured in whole seconds — the first move of a fight, or
         * one after the tab was away — says nothing about how fast the cursor is going
         * now.
         *
         * Outside that window the velocity is **zeroed, not kept.** Keeping the last
         * good reading looked more careful and was a bug with teeth: a cursor creeping a
         * pixel at a time still refreshes `scrub.at`, so the aim was led by a velocity
         * sampled from some flick seconds earlier and the cat missed by up to
         * `PREDICT_CAP` every single time. The harness found it as a fight that could not
         * be lost — the cat pounced for seventy seconds and never once landed. Not
         * knowing the speed has to mean assuming there isn't one.
         */
        if (scrub.at && dt > 0.004 && dt < 0.12) {
          scrub.vx = (e.clientX - scrub.x) / dt;
          scrub.vy = (e.clientY - scrub.y) / dt;
        } else {
          scrub.vx = 0;
          scrub.vy = 0;
        }
        scrub.at = now;
        scrub.x = e.clientX;
        scrub.y = e.clientY;
        scrub.inside = true;
        scrub.seen = now;
      },
      { passive: true },
    );

    /*
     * Touch (§5.2, answered in 1.2). **The whole input gap was this listener not existing.**
     *
     * `scrub.x/y` was fed only by `pointermove`, and the one thing a finger does that a mouse
     * cannot is arrive somewhere and then emit nothing at all. A finger placed and held
     * perfectly still fires `pointerdown` and no `pointermove`, so the position stayed at -1
     * and no hold could ever register. Everything else about the scrub was already portable,
     * because `stepScrub` polls the position every frame rather than reacting to events.
     *
     * Velocity is **zeroed, not sampled**. §5.3's aim leads the pointer, and a first touch has
     * no previous sample to lead from — the same reasoning the `dt` window above uses, arrived
     * at by the same bug. A finger also cannot be led anywhere: it does not travel, it lands.
     *
     * The anchor and the clock are reset too, so a new touch starts a fresh hold rather than
     * inheriting the last one's progress from wherever the previous finger was.
     */
    window.addEventListener(
      'pointerdown',
      (e) => {
        if (!arena.on) return;
        /*
         * A mouse press **clears** the touch record rather than ignoring it, so the most recent
         * press is what the `click` handler below reads. Returning early instead left `touch.at`
         * set forever after a single tap — and on a hybrid device (a touchscreen laptop, the one
         * machine where both inputs are real) every subsequent *mouse* click was then measured
         * against a timestamp from minutes earlier, failed the tap test, and silently stopped
         * throwing treats. §5.4 going quiet with no error is exactly the failure §5.4 forbids.
         */
        if (e.pointerType === 'mouse') {
          touch.at = 0;
          return;
        }
        const now = performance.now();
        scrub.x = scrub.ax = e.clientX;
        scrub.y = scrub.ay = e.clientY;
        scrub.vx = scrub.vy = 0;
        scrub.at = now;
        scrub.since = now;
        scrub.seen = now;
        scrub.inside = true;
        dropHold(scrub); // let `stepScrub` decide what is under the finger this frame
        touch.at = now;
        touch.onClaim = false;
      },
      { passive: true },
    );

    /*
     * Lifting a finger ends the hold, and is not a mouse leaving the window.
     *
     * `pointerout` with a null `relatedTarget` already fires on lift and already clears
     * `scrub.inside` — §5.3's "a cursor that leaves mid-leap is a whiff" turns out to describe
     * touch exactly, for free. What it does *not* do is release the hold anchor, so without
     * this the next touch could inherit a stale `since` and complete a scrub it never held.
     */
    const liftFinger = (e: PointerEvent) => {
      if (e.pointerType === 'mouse') return;
      scrub.x = scrub.y = -1;
      scrub.ax = scrub.ay = -1;
      // The finger has gone, so the hold has — and its mark goes with it, or the claim keeps a
      // half-full wash that `claim()` would snapshot as the style it arrived with (pillar 2).
      dropHold(scrub);
      scrub.inside = false;
      hideRing();
    };
    window.addEventListener('pointerup', liftFinger, { passive: true });
    window.addEventListener('pointercancel', liftFinger, { passive: true });

    /*
     * **A hold must not also be a click.** Found by a harness reporting `8 → 0 claims` from a
     * single hold, which is a win the fight had not earned — the fight had *navigated away*.
     *
     * §5.1 deliberately allows a claim *inside* a link: `PROTECTED` excludes `a` from being
     * claimed itself but not from containing one, because "a `.frame` inside a card link is fair
     * game — tilting it leaves the link focusable, clickable and exactly where it was". True on a
     * mouse, where dwelling on a link never clicks it. On touch a hold **ends in a click**, so the
     * core verb navigated off the page on most of a portfolio, where nearly everything is inside a
     * link. `touch-action` and `user-select` do nothing about this; only the click does.
     *
     * Capturing, and only for the click that *terminates a hold on a claim* — `isWorking`, which
     * reads a flag recorded during the hold rather than the DOM at click time. The first attempt
     * asked `e.target.closest('.cat-claimed')` and still navigated: a hold that *completes* frees
     * the element, so the check read false at precisely the moment it was needed. The question is
     * about the gesture, and the gesture is over by the time you can ask.
     *
     * A genuine tap on a claimed link still navigates, because §11 promises the page keeps
     * working. So the rule is exactly "working on a claim is not pressing it" — the same thing
     * the crosshair says on a desktop, except here it has to be enforced rather than drawn.
     */
    window.addEventListener(
      'click',
      (e) => {
        if (!arena.on || !touch.at) return;
        if (!isWorking(performance.now() - touch.at, touch.onClaim)) return;
        e.preventDefault();
        e.stopPropagation();
      },
      { capture: true },
    );

    /*
     * **A held finger on a claimed link is a link *drag* to the browser.**
     *
     * Found as `8 → 8 claims` from a hold that plainly should have taken one: not a click this
     * time but a drag. A long press on an `<a>` starts the native link-drag gesture, which fires
     * `pointercancel` — so the hold released itself a few hundred milliseconds in, every time,
     * only on claims that sit inside links. `touch-action` does not cover it (that governs
     * panning and zooming), and neither does `user-select` (that governs text).
     *
     * Refused here rather than with `-webkit-user-drag` in CSS, because the drag source is the
     * *link* and the claim is its descendant — the property would have to go on somebody else's
     * element, and this is one listener that stops when the fight does.
     */
    window.addEventListener('dragstart', (e) => {
      // Coarse pointers only. On a desktop a drag is deliberate — nobody drags an image by
      // accident — and dragging a figure out of a page is a real affordance §11 has no reason to
      // take away. The bug is specifically that a *long press* is indistinguishable from the start
      // of a link-drag, and long-press-as-drag is a touch behaviour.
      if (!arena.on || !coarse()) return;
      const t = e.target;
      if (t instanceof Element && t.closest('.cat-claimed')) e.preventDefault();
    });

    /*
     * Click to throw (§3, §5.4).
     *
     * Anything the page uses is left alone: a click on something `INTERACTIVE` — a link, a
     * button, a field — does what it always did, because §11 promises the page stays
     * usable and pillar 2 promises a recruiter can keep reading. So does a click in
     * the cat's own HUD, which is where the toggle lives. Everything else is a throw.
     *
     * `INTERACTIVE` and not `PROTECTED`: the latter includes `[tabindex]`, which matches
     * `<main tabindex="-1">` — so using it here meant no click inside the page content ever
     * threw anything, while the crosshair said it would. See the note in `arena.ts`.
     *
     * Not `preventDefault`ed anywhere: there is nothing to prevent on a paragraph,
     * and swallowing the event would take text selection with it.
     */
    window.addEventListener('click', (e) => {
      if (!arena.on) return;
      const t = e.target;
      if (t instanceof Element && (t.closest(INTERACTIVE) || t.closest('#cat-hud'))) return;
      /*
       * On touch, `click` also arrives when a *hold* ends — same gesture, same event — so the
       * throw has to ask whether this was a tap. `touch.at` is stamped by `pointerdown` and
       * cleared on a throw, so a zero here means the click came from a mouse and nothing changes
       * for the path that has worked since 0.5.
       */
      if (touch.at && !isTap(performance.now() - touch.at)) return;
      /*
       * §15's first order: **a click on a claim sends a kitten to it.**
       *
       * The disambiguation costs nothing because 1.2 already built it. `INTERACTIVE` above means a
       * click on a link still navigates (§11), and `claimUnder` is the same hit test the pointer
       * used to scrub with — so the two orders divide the page between them exactly the way the
       * visitor would guess: *point at the thing you want back, or throw somewhere empty to pull
       * the cat away.* Nothing needs teaching and nothing needs aiming.
       *
       * Ordering deliberately does **not** cost a treat. Treats are the throw's resource (§5.4) and
       * making the free order compete with them would put a price on the only thing a commander
       * does, in a mode whose whole premise is that doing nothing is allowed.
       */
      if (commanding()) {
        const claim = claimUnder(e.clientX, e.clientY);
        if (claim) {
          order(claim, e.clientX, e.clientY);
          touch.at = 0;
          return;
        }
      }
      throwTreat(e.clientX, e.clientY);
      touch.at = 0;
    });

    // The pointer leaving the window is not the same as the pointer holding still
    // (§5.3). `pointerout` with a null relatedTarget is the one that fires when it
    // leaves the document rather than merely crossing between elements.
    document.addEventListener('pointerout', (e) => {
      if (!(e as PointerEvent).relatedTarget) {
        scrub.inside = false;
        scrub.vx = scrub.vy = 0;
        hideRing();
      }
    });
    document.addEventListener('pointerover', () => (scrub.inside = true));

    // Esc, and only Esc: the truce keystroke has to work from anywhere, and the
    // arena adds no other binding (§11 — the site's own shortcuts stay owned by
    // the pages that define them).
    window.addEventListener('keydown', (e) => {
      // Somebody is at the keyboard, so somebody is here (§11 as amended in 2.0 — see the truce
      // in `tick`). Any key, including the ones this fight has no opinion about.
      scrub.seen = performance.now();
      // `want`, not `on`: Esc during the flood is the clearest case there is of an
      // interruption that must not have to wait for an animation to finish.
      if (e.key === 'Escape' && arena.want) truce();
    });

    /*
     * **Scrolling is presence, and until 2.0 it was not.**
     *
     * §11's truce clock was refreshed by `pointermove` alone, which made a very specific lie
     * possible: a visitor reading with a scroll wheel and never moving the mouse looked, to the
     * game, exactly like a visitor who had left. 1.3 met this from the other side — its harness
     * wheeled the page for twenty seconds and the fight truced underneath it, and the note in the
     * changelog fixed the *harness* by scrolling a different way. That was the wrong half to fix.
     *
     * It matters much more in commander mode, where reading while the squad works is not just
     * allowed but the intended way to play. Passive, because this must never delay a scroll.
     */
    window.addEventListener('scroll', () => (scrub.seen = performance.now()), { passive: true });

    // Tab hidden long enough ends it: nobody comes back to a page mid-invasion.
    let hiddenAt = 0;
    document.addEventListener('visibilitychange', () => {
      if (document.hidden) {
        hiddenAt = Date.now();
        return;
      }
      if (arena.on && hiddenAt && Date.now() - hiddenAt > HIDDEN_TRUCE_MS) endQuietly();
    });

    // Reduce-motion can be flipped mid-visit from either source (the OS query or
    // the site's own toggle). Turning it on ends a running fight, because there
    // is no still version of this yet.
    const reconsider = () => {
      if (arena.on && blockedBecause()) endQuietly();
      else syncToggle();
    };
    matchMedia('(prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)').addEventListener('change', reconsider);
    new MutationObserver(reconsider).observe(document.documentElement, {
      attributes: true,
      attributeFilter: ['class'],
    });

    syncToggle();
  }

  document.addEventListener('astro:page-load', () => {
    init();
    // A curtain caught mid-flight by a navigation has nothing left to reveal.
    arena.curtain?.();
    arena.curtain = null;
    stopCurtain();
    // A fight does not survive a page change yet: the old board's elements are
    // gone, and step 1 has nothing worth carrying across. Re-querying the new
    // page (§13.6) waits for the build that has something to carry.
    if (arena.on) endQuietly();
    syncToggle();
  });
</script>
